      "a": 3,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 5th April Evening Shift. Correct option: D. According to Ohm's law, the resistance R is given by the ratio of potential difference V to the current I :\n\n$$ \\mathrm{R}=\\frac{\\mathrm{V}}{\\mathrm{I}} $$\n\nThe reading of voltmeter is $\\mathrm{V}=10 \\mathrm{~V}$ and that of ammeter is $\\mathrm{I}=5 \\mathrm{~A}$\n\nThen resistance is $\\mathrm{R}=\\frac{10}{5}=2 \\Omega$\n\nWhen a quantity is determined by the division of two other quantities $\\left(\\mathrm{R}=\\frac{\\mathrm{V}}{\\mathrm{I}}\\right)$, the relative error in the result is the sum of the relative errors of the individual quantities.\n\nThe relative error is,\n\n$$ \\frac{\\Delta \\mathrm{R}}{\\mathrm{R}}=\\frac{\\Delta \\mathrm{V}}{\\mathrm{~V}}+\\frac{\\Delta \\mathrm{I}}{\\mathrm{I}} $$\n\nWhere $\\Delta \\mathrm{R}$ is the absolute error in resistance, $\\Delta \\mathrm{V}$ is the error in voltage, and $\\Delta \\mathrm{I}$ is the error in current.\n\nThe error in a measurement is taken as the least count of the instrument used.\n\nSo, error in voltmeter is $\\Delta \\mathrm{V}=500 \\mathrm{mV}=0.5 \\mathrm{~V}$\n\nAnd the error in ammeter is $\\Delta \\mathrm{I}=200 \\mathrm{~mA}=0.2 \\mathrm{~A}$\n\nSubstituting values into the error equation\n\n$$ \\frac{\\Delta \\mathrm{R}}{2}=\\frac{0.5}{10}+\\frac{0.2}{5} $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $\\frac{\\Delta \\mathrm{R}}{2}=0.05+0.04=0.09$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\Delta \\mathrm{R}=0.09 \\times 2=0.18 \\Omega $$\n\nTherefore, the estimated error in the resistance measurement is $0.18 \\Omega$.\n\nHence, the correct option is (D).",
