      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 5th April Morning Shift. Correct option: A. Electric power $(\\mathrm{P})$ is defined as work done per unit time. In a resistor of resistance R if the current is I , then electric power is given as,\n\n$$ \\mathrm{P}=\\mathrm{I}^2 \\mathrm{R} $$\n\nFor the dimensions of force (F) $=$ Mass × Acceleration $=\\left[\\mathrm{MLT}^{-2}\\right]$\n\nFor the dimensions of work $(\\mathrm{W})=$ Force × Distance $=\\left[\\mathrm{MLT}^{-2}\\right] \\times[\\mathrm{L}]=\\left[\\mathrm{ML}^2 \\mathrm{~T}^{-2}\\right]$\n\nHence, the dimensions of power are,\n\n$$ [\\mathrm{P}]=\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{W}}{\\mathrm{~T}}\\right]=\\frac{\\left[\\mathrm{ML}^2 \\mathrm{~T}^{-2}\\right]}{[\\mathrm{T}]}=\\left[\\mathrm{ML}^2 \\mathrm{~T}^{-3}\\right] $$\n\nFrom the electric power formula,\n\n$$ \\mathrm{R}=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}}{\\mathrm{I}^2} $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ [\\mathrm{R}]=\\frac{\\left[\\mathrm{ML}^2 \\mathrm{~T}^{-3}\\right]}{\\left[\\mathrm{A}^2\\right]}=\\left[\\mathrm{ML}^2 \\mathrm{~T}^{-3} \\mathrm{~A}^{-2}\\right] $$\n\nThe energy (E) stored in a capacitor is E $=\\frac{1}{2} C V^2$.\n\nElectric Potential (V) is defined as work per unit charge, $\\mathrm{V}=\\frac{\\mathrm{W}}{\\mathrm{Q}}$,\n\nThe dimensions of charge ( Q ) is $[\\mathrm{Q}]=[\\mathrm{AT}]$.\n\nSo, the dimensions of potential is, $[\\mathrm{V}]=\\frac{\\left[\\mathrm{ML}^2 \\mathrm{~T}^{-2}\\right]}{[\\mathrm{AT}]}=\\left[\\mathrm{ML}^2 \\mathrm{~T}^{-3} \\mathrm{~A}^{-1}\\right]$\n\nUsing the definition of capacitance $\\mathrm{C}=\\frac{\\mathrm{Q}}{\\mathrm{V}}$, the dimensions of capacitance is;\n\n$$ [C]=\\frac{[A T]}{\\left[{M L^2}^{-3} A^{-1}\\right]}=\\left[M^{-1} L^{-2} T^4 A^2\\right] $$\n\nThe energy (E) stored in an inductor is E $=\\frac{1}{2} \\mathrm{LI}^2$.\n\nThe dimensions of energy are $\\left[\\mathrm{ML}^2 \\mathrm{~T}^{-2}\\right]$.\n\nSo, the dimensions of inductance are,\n\n$$ \\mathrm{L}=\\frac{2 \\mathrm{E}}{\\mathrm{I}^2} $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ [\\mathrm{L}]=\\frac{\\left[\\mathrm{ML}^2 \\mathrm{~T}^{-2}\\right]}{\\mathrm{A}^2}=\\left[\\mathrm{ML}^2 \\mathrm{~T}^{-2} \\mathrm{~A}^{-2}\\right] $$\n\nHence, the dimensions of inductance, capacitance, and resistance are,\n\n$[\\mathrm{R}]=\\left[\\mathrm{ML}^2 \\mathrm{~T}^{-3} \\mathrm{~A}^{-2}\\right],[\\mathrm{C}]= \\left[\\mathrm{M}^{-1} \\mathrm{~L}^{-2} \\mathrm{~T}^4 \\mathrm{~A}^2\\right],[\\mathrm{L}]=\\left[\\mathrm{ML}^2 \\mathrm{~T}^{-2} \\mathrm{~A}^{-2}\\right]$\n\nLet the dimensional formula [ $\\mathrm{ML}^2 \\mathrm{~T}^{-4} \\mathrm{~A}^{-2}$ ] corresponds to $\\mathrm{R}^{\\mathrm{x}} \\mathrm{L}^y \\mathrm{C}^z$\n\nSubstituting these into the equation :\n\n$\\left[M^1 L^2 T^{-4} A^{-2}\\right]=\\left[M L^2 T^{-3} A^{-2}\\right]^x\\left[M L^2 T^{-2} A^{-2}\\right]^y\\left[M^{-1} L^{-2} T^4 A^2\\right]^z$\n\n$$ \\left[M^1 L^2 T^{-4} A^{-2}\\right]=\\left[M^{x+y-z} L^{2 x+2 y-2 z} T^{-3 x-2 y+4 z} A^{-2 x-2 y+2 z}\\right] $$\n\nComparing the exponents for each fundamental unit:\n\nFor mass, $\\mathrm{M}: \\mathrm{x}+\\mathrm{y}-\\mathrm{z}=1 \\ldots$ (i)\n\nFor length, $\\mathrm{L}: 2 \\mathrm{x}+2 \\mathrm{y}-2 \\mathrm{z}=2 \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{x}+\\mathrm{y}-\\mathrm{z}=1 \\ldots$ (ii)\n\nFor time, $\\mathrm{T}:-3 \\mathrm{x}-2 \\mathrm{y}+4 \\mathrm{z}=-4 \\ldots$ (iii)\n\nFor current, $\\mathrm{A}:-2 \\mathrm{x}-2 \\mathrm{y}+2 \\mathrm{z}=-2 \\ldots$ (iv)\n\nFrom (i): $\\mathrm{x}+\\mathrm{y}=1+\\mathrm{z}$\n\nSubstituting this into (ii) :\n\n$$ -3 x-2 y+4 z=-4 $$\n\n$-x-2(x+y)+4 z=-4$\n\n$$ \\begin{array}{r} -x-2(1+z)+4 z=-4 \\\\ -x-2-2 z+4 z=-4 \\\\ -x+2 z=-2 \\Rightarrow x=2 z+2 \\ldots (v) \\end{array} $$\n\nFrom given options the possible exponents of resistance (R) are 1 (a and b), $-\\frac{1}{2}$ (c) or -1 (d).\n\nIf $\\mathrm{x}=1, \\mathrm{z}=\\frac{(\\mathrm{x}-2)}{2}=\\frac{(1-2)}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}$; then $\\mathrm{x}+\\mathrm{y}=1+\\mathrm{z} \\Rightarrow 1+\\mathrm{y}=1-\\frac{1}{2} \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{y}=-\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nThen the expression will be $\\mathrm{R}^{\\mathrm{x}} \\mathrm{L}^{\\mathrm{y}} \\mathrm{C}^{\\mathrm{z}}=\\mathrm{R}^1 \\mathrm{~L}^{-1 / 2} \\mathrm{C}^{-1 / 2}=\\frac{\\mathrm{R}}{\\sqrt{\\mathrm{LC}}} \\rightarrow$ (a)\n\nIf $\\mathrm{x}=-\\frac{1}{2}, \\mathrm{z}=\\frac{(\\mathrm{x}-2)}{2}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}-2\\right)}{2}=-\\frac{5}{4}$; then $\\mathrm{x}+\\mathrm{y}=1+\\mathrm{z} \\Rightarrow-\\frac{1}{2}+\\mathrm{y}=1-\\frac{5}{2} \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{y}=-1$\n\nThen the expression will be $\\mathrm{R}^{\\mathrm{x}} \\mathrm{L}^{\\mathrm{y}} \\mathrm{C}^{\\mathrm{z}}=\\mathrm{R}^{-1 / 2} \\mathrm{~L}^{-1} \\mathrm{C}^{-5 / 4}$ (doesn't match option (c))\n\nIf $x=-1, z=\\frac{(x-2)}{2}=\\frac{(-1-2)}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}$; then $x+y=1+z \\Rightarrow-1+y=1-\\frac{3}{2} \\Rightarrow y=\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nThen the expression will be $\\mathrm{R}^{\\mathrm{x}} \\mathrm{L}^{\\mathrm{y}} \\mathrm{C}^{\\mathrm{z}}=\\mathrm{R}^{-1} \\mathrm{~L}^{1 / 2} \\mathrm{C}^{1 / 2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{\\mathrm{LC}}}{\\mathrm{R}}$ (doesn't match option (d))\n\nTherefore, the dimensional formula [ $\\mathrm{M}^1 \\mathrm{~L}^2 \\mathrm{~T}^{-4} \\mathrm{~A}^{-2}$ ] corresponds to $\\frac{\\mathrm{R}}{\\sqrt{\\mathrm{LC}}}$.",
