      "a": 1,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Evening Shift. Correct option: B. To find the dimensions of the universal gravitational constant G in terms of Planck's constant h , length L , mass M , and time T , we must first determine the base dimensions of both G and h in the standard MLT system.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the force F between two masses $\\mathrm{m}_1$ and $\\mathrm{m}_2$ separated by a distance $r$ is given by:\n\n$$ \\mathrm{F}=\\frac{\\mathrm{Gm}_1 \\mathrm{~m}_2}{\\mathrm{r}^2} $$\n\n$$ \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{G}=\\frac{\\mathrm{Fr}^2}{\\mathrm{~m}_1 \\mathrm{~m}_2} $$\n\nSubstituting the dimensions $\\left[M L T^{-2}\\right]$ for force, $[L]$ for distance $(r)$, and mass( $m$ ) as $[M]$ :\n\n$$ [\\mathrm{G}]=\\frac{\\left[\\mathrm{MLT}^{-2}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{L}^2\\right]}{[\\mathrm{M}][\\mathrm{M}]}=\\left[\\mathrm{M}^{-1} \\mathrm{~L}^3 \\mathrm{~T}^{-2}\\right] $$\n\nPlanck's constant relates the energy $E$ of a photon to its frequency $v$ via the equation:\n\n$$ \\mathrm{E}=\\mathrm{h} \\nu $$\n\nSince frequency $v$ has dimensions of inverse time [ $\\mathrm{T}^{-1}$ ], and energy E (work done) has dimensions $\\left[\\mathrm{ML}^2 \\mathrm{~T}^{-2}\\right]$, we have:\n\n$$ [\\mathrm{h}]=\\frac{[\\mathrm{E}]}{[\\mathrm{v}]}=\\frac{\\left[\\mathrm{ML}^2 \\mathrm{~T}^{-2}\\right]}{\\left[\\mathrm{T}^{-1}\\right]}=\\left[\\mathrm{ML}^2 \\mathrm{~T}^{-1}\\right] $$\n\nLet assume the relationship is of the form $\\mathrm{G}=\\mathrm{h}^{\\mathrm{a}} \\mathrm{L}^{\\mathrm{b}} \\mathrm{M}^{\\mathrm{c}} \\mathrm{T}^{\\mathrm{d}}$. Comparing the dimensions:\n\n$$ \\left[\\mathrm{M}^{-1} \\mathrm{~L}^3 \\mathrm{~T}^{-2}\\right]=\\left[\\mathrm{ML}^2 \\mathrm{~T}^{-1}\\right]^{\\mathrm{a}}[\\mathrm{~L}]^{\\mathrm{b}}[\\mathrm{M}]^{\\mathrm{c}}[\\mathrm{~T}]^{\\mathrm{d}} $$\n\n$$ \\left[\\mathrm{M}^{-1} \\mathrm{~L}^3 \\mathrm{~T}^{-2}\\right]=\\left[\\mathrm{M}^{\\mathrm{a}+\\mathrm{c}} \\mathrm{~L}^{2 \\mathrm{a}+\\mathrm{b}} \\mathrm{~T}^{-\\mathrm{a}+\\mathrm{d}}\\right] $$\n\nBy equating the exponents of $\\mathrm{M}, \\mathrm{L}$, and T from both sides, we get a system of equations:\n\nFor $\\mathrm{M}: \\mathrm{a}+\\mathrm{c}=-1 \\ldots$ (i)\n\nFor $\\mathrm{L}: 2 \\mathrm{a}+\\mathrm{b}=3 \\ldots$ (ii)\n\nFor $\\mathrm{T}:-\\mathrm{a}+\\mathrm{d}=-2 \\ldots$ (iii)\n\nLooking at the provided options, we see that h is with a power of $1, \\mathrm{a}=1$\n\nLet's $\\mathrm{a}=1$ :\n\nFrom eq (i): $1+\\mathrm{c}=-1 \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{c}=-2$\n\nFrom eq (ii): $2(1)+b=3 \\Rightarrow b=1$\n\nFrom eq (iii): $-1+\\mathrm{d}=-2 \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{~d}=-1$\n\nSubstituting these values, we get $G \\propto h^1 L^1 M^{-2} T^{-1}$. This corresponds to the dimensional formula $\\left[\\mathrm{hT}^{-1} \\mathrm{LM}^{-2}\\right]$.\n\nHence, the correct option is (B).",
      "source": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Evening Shift | Mathematics in Physics (Vectors, Calculus Basics) | mnrqi2lu",
      "pyq": true,
      "exactPaperText": true,
      "pyqSourceSlug": "units-and-measurements"
