      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Morning Shift. Correct option: C. For smooth inclined plane :\n\nWhen the plane is smooth, there is no friction. The only forces acting on the block along the incline are the component of gravity ( $\\mathrm{mg} \\sin \\theta$ ).\n\n[Diagram: JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Morning Shift Physics - Laws of Motion Question 4 English Explanation 1]\n\nAccording to Newton's Second Law :\n\n$$ \\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{net}}=\\mathrm{ma} $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $\\mathrm{mg} \\sin \\theta=\\mathrm{ma}_{\\mathrm{s}}$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\mathrm{a}_{\\mathrm{s}}=\\mathrm{g} \\sin \\theta $$\n\nUsing the second equation of motion, $S=u t+\\frac{1}{2} a t^2$, where the block starts from rest $(u=0)$ and travels a distance L (length of the incline):\n\n$$ \\mathrm{L}=\\frac{1}{2} \\mathrm{a}_{\\mathrm{s}} \\mathrm{t}_{\\mathrm{s}}^2 $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ t_s=\\sqrt{\\frac{2 L}{a_s}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{2 L}{g \\sin \\theta}} $$\n\nFor rough inclined plane :\n\nOn a rough plane, kinetic friction ( $\\mathrm{f}_{\\mathrm{k}}$ ) acts upwards against the motion.\n\n[Diagram: JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Morning Shift Physics - Laws of Motion Question 4 English Explanation 2]\n\nThe normal force is $\\mathrm{N}=\\mathrm{mg} \\cos \\theta$, so $\\mathrm{f}_{\\mathrm{k}}=\\mu_{\\mathrm{k}} \\mathrm{N}=\\mu_{\\mathrm{k}} \\mathrm{mg} \\cos \\theta$.\n\nThe net force along the incline is :\n\n$$ \\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{net}}=\\mathrm{mg} \\sin \\theta-\\mu_{\\mathrm{k}} \\mathrm{mg} \\cos \\theta $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $\\mathrm{ma}_{\\mathrm{r}}=\\mathrm{m}\\left(\\mathrm{g} \\sin \\theta-\\mu_{\\mathrm{k}} \\mathrm{g} \\cos \\theta\\right)$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\mathrm{a}_{\\mathrm{r}}=\\mathrm{g}\\left(\\sin \\theta-\\mu_{\\mathrm{k}} \\cos \\theta\\right) $$\n\nThe time taken $\\mathrm{t}_{\\mathrm{r}}$ to travel the same distance L is:\n\n$$ t_r=\\sqrt{\\frac{2 L}{a_r}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{2 L}{g\\left(\\sin \\theta-\\mu_k \\cos \\theta\\right)}} $$\n\nIt is given that $\\mathrm{t}_{\\mathrm{r}}$ is $50 \\%$ more than $\\mathrm{t}_{\\mathrm{s}}$.\n\n$$ \\mathrm{t}_{\\mathrm{r}}=\\mathrm{t}_{\\mathrm{s}}+0.5 \\mathrm{t}_{\\mathrm{s}}=1.5 \\mathrm{t}_{\\mathrm{s}}=\\frac{3}{2} \\mathrm{t}_{\\mathrm{s}} $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\mathrm{t}_{\\mathrm{r}}^2=\\frac{9}{4} \\mathrm{t}_{\\mathrm{s}}^2 $$\n\nSubstituting the expressions for $\\mathrm{t}_{\\mathrm{r}}^2$ and $\\mathrm{t}_{\\mathrm{s}}^2$ :\n\n$$ \\frac{2 L}{g\\left(\\sin \\theta-\\mu_k \\cos \\theta\\right)}=\\frac{9}{4}\\left(\\frac{2 L}{g \\sin \\theta}\\right) $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $\\frac{1}{\\sin \\theta-\\mu_k \\cos \\theta}=\\frac{9}{4 \\sin \\theta}$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $4 \\sin \\theta=9 \\sin \\theta-9 \\mu_{\\mathrm{k}} \\cos \\theta$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $9 \\mu_{\\mathrm{k}} \\cos \\theta=5 \\sin \\theta$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\mu_{\\mathrm{k}}=\\frac{5}{9} \\tan \\theta $$\n\nThe angle of inclination is $\\theta=45^{\\circ}$\n\n$$ \\mu_{\\mathrm{k}}=\\frac{5}{9} \\tan 45^{\\circ}=\\frac{5}{9} $$\n\nTherefore, the correct option is (C).",
      "source": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Morning Shift | Center of Mass, Momentum and Collision | mnswhrgg",
      "pyq": true,
      "exactPaperText": true,
