      "a": 1,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Evening Shift. Correct option: B. When the bubble rises steadily, the net force is zero. The forces involved are :\n\n[Diagram: JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Evening Shift Physics - Properties of Matter Question 18 English Explanation]\n\nBuoyant force $\\left(\\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{B}}\\right)$ acting upwards: $\\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{B}}=\\mathrm{V} \\sigma \\mathrm{g}$, where $\\sigma$ is liquid density and V is the volume of the bubble.\n\nWeight ( $\\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{g}}$ ) acting downwards: $\\mathrm{W}=\\mathrm{V} \\rho \\mathrm{g}$, where $\\rho$ is air density.\n\nViscous drag $\\left(\\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{v}}\\right)$ acting downwards (opposing motion) :\n\n$\\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{v}}=6 \\pi \\eta \\mathrm{rv}$, where $\\eta$ is the viscosity of liquid, r is the radius of body and v is the speed.\n\nAs the bubble is rising steadily, so the net force acting on it should be zero.\n\nEquating the forces :\n\n$$ \\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{B}}=\\mathrm{W}+\\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{v}} $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ V \\sigma \\mathrm{~g}=V \\rho \\mathrm{~g}+6 \\pi \\eta \\mathrm{rv} $$\n\nSince the density of air ( $\\rho$ ) is much smaller than the density of the liquid ( $\\sigma=2000 \\mathrm{~kg} / \\mathrm{m}^3$ ), we can neglect W :\n\n$$ \\frac{4}{3} \\pi r^3 \\sigma g=6 \\pi \\eta r v $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\eta=\\frac{2 r^2 \\sigma g}{9 v} $$\n\nThe radius of bubble is $\\mathrm{r}=\\frac{2 \\mathrm{~mm}}{2}=1 \\mathrm{~mm}=10^{-3} \\mathrm{~m}$\n\nThe density of liquid is $\\sigma=2000 \\mathrm{~kg} / \\mathrm{m}^3$\n\nThe constant speed of rise of bubble is $\\mathrm{v}=0.5 \\mathrm{~cm} / \\mathrm{s}=0.005 \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s}=5 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s}$\n\nAnd the acceleration due to gravity is $\\mathrm{g}=10 \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s}^2$\n\nPutting the values,\n\n$$ \\eta=\\frac{2 \\times\\left(10^{-3}\\right)^2 \\times 2000 \\times 10}{9 \\times 5 \\times 10^{-3}} $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $\\eta=\\frac{2 \\times 10^{-6} \\times 2 \\times 10^4}{45 \\times 10^{-3}}=\\frac{4 \\times 10^{-2}}{45 \\times 10^{-3}}$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\eta=\\frac{40}{45} \\approx 0.888 \\mathrm{~Pa} \\cdot \\mathrm{~s} $$\n\nAs Pa.s is the SI unit of viscosity and the required value is in CGS unit, Poise;\n\n$$ 1 \\mathrm{~Pa} \\cdot \\mathrm{~s}=10 \\text { Poise } \\Rightarrow \\eta=0.888 \\times 10=8.88 \\text { Poise } $$\n\nTherefore, the correct option is (B).",
