      "a": 0,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Evening Shift. Correct option: A. Let the node between $\\mathrm{C}_1$ and $\\mathrm{C}_2$ be X ,\n\n[Diagram: JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Evening Shift Physics - Capacitor Question 1 English Explanation 1]\n\nThe wire (short circuit) connecting terminal A to node between $\\mathrm{C}_2$ and $\\mathrm{C}_3$. Thus, this node is at the same potential as A.\n\nSo, capacitor $\\mathrm{C}_1$ is connected between A and X .\n\nThe capacitor $\\mathrm{C}_2$ is connected between A and X .\n\nThe capacitor $C_3$ is connected between $A$ and $B$.\n\nThe capacitor $\\mathrm{C}_4$ is connected between X and B .\n\nSo, the simplified circuit is,\n\n[Diagram: JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Evening Shift Physics - Capacitor Question 1 English Explanation 2]\n\nGiven values of capacitances are, $\\mathrm{C}_1=1 \\mu \\mathrm{~F}, \\mathrm{C}_2=1 \\mu \\mathrm{~F}, \\mathrm{C}_3=1 \\mu \\mathrm{~F}$ and $\\mathrm{C}_4=2 \\mu \\mathrm{~F}$\n\nBoth $\\mathrm{C}_1$ and $\\mathrm{C}_2$ are connected between point A and point X in parallel.\n\nThe equivalent capacitance $\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{AX}}$ is :\n\n$$ C_{A X}=C_1+C_2 $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ C_{A X}=1 \\mu F+1 \\mu F=2 \\mu F $$\n\nNow, the combination $\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{AX}}$ is in series with $\\mathrm{C}_4$ because the charge must flow through $\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{AX}}$ to reach $\\mathrm{C}_4$. So, the equivalent capacitance ( $\\mathrm{C}^{\\prime}$ ) is\n\n$$ \\frac{1}{\\mathrm{C}^{\\prime}}=\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{AX}}}+\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{C}_4} $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{C}^{\\prime}}=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\mathrm{C}^{\\prime}=1 \\mu \\mathrm{~F} $$\n\nThe entire top branch ( $C^{\\prime}$ ) is now in parallel with $C_3$, as both are connected across the main terminals $A$ and $B$.\n\nThe total equivalent capacitance $\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{eq}}$ is :\n\n$$ C_{\\mathrm{eq}}=C^{\\prime}+C_3 $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{eq}}=1 \\mu \\mathrm{~F}+1 \\mu \\mathrm{~F}$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{eq}}=2 \\mu \\mathrm{~F} $$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent capacitance between A and B is $2 \\mu \\mathrm{~F}$.\n\nThus, the correct option is (A).",
