      "a": 1,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Evening Shift. Correct option: B. In an LCR circuit the phase difference $\\phi$ is given by the formula :\n\n$$ \\tan \\phi=\\frac{\\left|\\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{L}}-\\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{C}}\\right|}{\\mathrm{R}} $$\n\nCase 1 : $\\mathrm{S}_1$ closed, $\\mathrm{S}_2$ open\n\n[Diagram: JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Evening Shift Physics - Alternating Current Question 5 English Explanation 1]\n\nIn this case the inductor $\\mathrm{L}_2$ is short-circuited. The active components are $\\mathrm{R}, \\mathrm{C}$, and $\\mathrm{L}_1$.\n\n$$ \\tan 30^{\\circ}=\\frac{\\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{L} 1}-\\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{C}}}{\\mathrm{R}} $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\omega \\mathrm{L}_1-\\frac{1}{\\omega \\mathrm{C}}}{\\mathrm{R}}$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$\\mathrm{R}=\\sqrt{3}\\left(\\omega \\mathrm{~L}_1-\\frac{1}{\\omega \\mathrm{C}}\\right) ......(ii)$$\n\nCase 2 : $S_2$ closed, $S_1$ open\n\n[Diagram: JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Evening Shift Physics - Alternating Current Question 5 English Explanation 2]\n\nHere, the inductor $L_1$ is short-circuited. The active components are $R, C$, and $L_2$.\n\n$$ \\tan 60^{\\circ}=\\frac{X_{L 2}-X_C}{R} $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $\\sqrt{3}=\\frac{\\omega \\mathrm{L}_2-\\frac{1}{\\omega \\mathrm{C}}}{\\mathrm{R}}$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\mathrm{R}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\left(\\omega \\mathrm{~L}_2-\\frac{1}{\\omega \\mathrm{C}}\\right) .......(ii)$$\n\nEquating both expressions for R :\n\n$$ \\sqrt{3}\\left(\\omega \\mathrm{~L}_1-\\frac{1}{\\omega \\mathrm{C}}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\left(\\omega \\mathrm{~L}_2-\\frac{1}{\\omega \\mathrm{C}}\\right) $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $3 \\omega L_1-\\frac{3}{\\omega C}=\\omega L_2-\\frac{1}{\\omega C}$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $3 \\omega L_1-\\omega L_2=\\frac{2}{\\omega C}$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $\\omega\\left(3 \\mathrm{~L}_1-\\mathrm{L}_2\\right)=\\frac{2}{\\omega \\mathrm{C}}$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ 3 L_1-L_2=\\frac{2}{\\omega^2 C} $$\n\nSubstituting $\\omega=300$ and $\\mathrm{C}=100 \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{~F}$ :\n\n$$ 3 \\mathrm{~L}_1-\\mathrm{L}_2=\\frac{2}{(300)^2 \\times 100 \\times 10^{-6}} $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ 3 \\mathrm{~L}_1-\\mathrm{L}_2=\\frac{2}{90000 \\times 10^{-4}}=\\frac{2}{9} $$\n\nTherefore, the value of $3 L_1-L_2$ is $\\frac{2}{9}$.\n\nHence, the correct option is (B).",
