      "a": 0,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Morning Shift. Correct option: A. The Einstein's photoelectric equation states that the energy of incident photons to the work function of the metal and the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted electrons.\n\n$$ \\mathrm{K}_{\\max }=\\mathrm{h} \\nu-\\Phi_0 $$\n\nWhere $\\mathrm{K}_{\\max }$ is the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectrons, $\\mathrm{h} \\nu$ is the energy of the incident photon (where h is Planck's constant and $\\nu$ is frequency) and $\\Phi_0$ is the work function of the metal, which is the minimum energy required to eject an electron.\n\nThe work function can also be expressed in terms of the threshold frequency $\\left(v_0\\right)$, which is the minimum frequency required for photoelectric emission:\n\n$$ \\Phi_0=\\mathrm{h} v_0 $$\n\nThe stopping potential ( $\\mathrm{V}_0$ ) is the negative potential applied to stop the most energetic photoelectrons. The work done by this potential equals the maximum kinetic energy:\n\n$$ \\mathrm{K}_{\\max }=\\mathrm{eV}_0 $$\n\nSubstituting this into the photoelectric equation:\n\n$$ \\mathrm{eV}_0=\\mathrm{h} v-\\phi \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{V}_0=\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{h}}{\\mathrm{e}}\\right) v-\\frac{\\Phi_0}{\\mathrm{e}} $$\n\nThe equation $\\mathrm{V}_0=\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{h}}{\\mathrm{e}}\\right) v-\\Phi_0$ is in the form of a straight line $(\\mathrm{y}=\\mathrm{mx}+\\mathrm{c})$.\n\nThe slope $\\mathrm{m}=\\frac{\\mathrm{h}}{\\mathrm{e}}=$ constant for all metals, which is why the lines are parallel.\n\nX -intercept represents the threshold frequency $\\left(v_0\\right)$, where $\\mathrm{V}_0=0$. At this frequency, the incident energy exactly equals the work function $\\left(\\Phi_0=\\mathrm{h} \\nu_0\\right)$.\n\nFrom the graph, we can identify the threshold frequencies for each metal:\n\nFor $\\mathrm{X}_1: v_0 \\approx 1.0 \\times 10^{14} \\mathrm{~Hz}$\n\nFor $\\mathrm{X}_2: v_0 \\approx 1.5 \\times 10^{14} \\mathrm{~Hz}$\n\nFor $\\mathrm{X}_3: v_0 \\approx 2.0 \\times 10^{14} \\mathrm{~Hz}$\n\nWe can see that $v_{01} Now, considering incident radiation of the same wavelength ( $\\lambda$ ).\n\nSince energy $E=\\frac{h c}{\\lambda}$, the incident energy is the same for all three metals.\n\nSubstituting this into the energy equation:\n\n$$ \\mathrm{K}_{\\max }=\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {incident }}-\\Phi_0 $$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {incident }}$ is constant, the metal with the smallest work function ( $\\Phi_0$ ) will result in the greatest kinetic energy ( $\\mathrm{K}_{\\text {max }}$ ).\n\nAs metal $\\mathrm{X}_1$ has the lowest threshold frequency and thus the lowest work function, it will emit electrons with the greatest kinetic energy.\n\nHence, the correct option is (A).",
      "source": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Morning Shift | Dual Nature of Matter | mnswhuty",
      "pyq": true,
