      "a": 2,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 5th April Morning Shift. Correct option: C. The radius of an electron in the $\\mathrm{n}^{\\text {th }}$ orbit of a hydrogen atom is given by the formula:\n\n$$ r_n=\\left(\\frac{\\epsilon_0 h^2}{\\pi m e^2}\\right) n^2 \\Rightarrow r_n \\propto n^2 $$\n\nThe ratio of radii of $i^{\\text {th }}$ orbit to $f^{\\text {th }}$ orbit is $r_i: r_f=16: 4$,\n\n$$ \\frac{\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{i}}^2}{\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{f}}^2}=\\frac{16}{4}=4 $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\frac{\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{i}}}{\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{f}}}=2 \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{i}}=2 \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{f}} $$\n\nFor a transition to a lower orbit in a hydrogen atom, the simplest integers satisfying this are $\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{f}}=2$ and $\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{i}}=$ 4.\n\nThe wavelength of the emitted photon is given by the Rydberg formula:\n\n$$ \\frac{1}{\\lambda}=\\mathrm{R}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{f}}^2}-\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{i}}^2}\\right) $$\n\nSubstituting the values $\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{f}}=2$ and $\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{i}}=4$ :\n\n$$ \\frac{1}{\\lambda}=\\mathrm{R}\\left(\\frac{1}{2^2}-\\frac{1}{4^2}\\right)=\\mathrm{R}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{16}\\right) $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $\\frac{1}{\\lambda}=\\mathrm{R}\\left(\\frac{4-1}{16}\\right)=\\frac{3 \\mathrm{R}}{16}$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\lambda=\\frac{16}{3 R} $$\n\nPutting the value of Rydberg's constant $\\mathrm{R}=1.0973 \\times 10^7 \\mathrm{~m}^{-1}$\n\n$$ \\lambda=\\frac{16}{3 \\times 1.0973 \\times 10^7} \\approx 4.86 \\times 10^{-7} \\mathrm{~m}=486 \\times 10^{-9} \\mathrm{~m} $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\lambda=486 \\mathrm{~nm} $$\n\nTherefore, the wavelength of photon emitted due to this transition is 486 nm .\n\nHence, the correct option is (C).",
      "source": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 5th April Morning Shift | Atomic Physics | mnswhrq7",
