      "a": 2,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Morning Shift. Correct option: C. In nuclear reactions, the energy released or absorbed ( $\\Delta \\mathrm{Mc}^2$ or Q -value) is the difference between the total binding energy of the product nuclei and the total binding energy of the reactant nuclei.\n\n$$ 2 X^3+Y^4 \\rightarrow Z^{10}+Q $$\n\nThe reactants consist of two nuclei of mass number $\\mathrm{A}=3$ and one nucleus of mass number $\\mathrm{A}=4$.\n\nBinding energy ( BE) is calculated as, $\\mathrm{BE}=($ Binding Energy per nucleon $) \\times($ Mass Number $)$.\n\nFor one nucleus of $\\mathrm{A}=3: \\mathrm{BE}_3=3 \\times 5.6 \\mathrm{MeV}=16.8 \\mathrm{MeV}$\n\nFor two such nuclei: $2 \\times \\mathrm{BE}_3=33.6 \\mathrm{MeV}$\n\nFor one nucleus of $\\mathrm{A}=4: \\mathrm{BE}_4=4 \\times 7.4 \\mathrm{MeV}=29.6 \\mathrm{MeV}$\n\nTotal BE of reactants $=33.6+29.6=63.2 \\mathrm{MeV}$\n\nThe product is one nucleus of mass number $\\mathrm{A}=10$.\n\nFor one nucleus of $\\mathrm{A}=10: \\mathrm{BE}_{10}=10 \\times 6.1 \\mathrm{MeV}=61.0 \\mathrm{MeV}$\n\nThe energy released or the change in mass-energy is :\n\n$$ \\Delta \\mathrm{Mc}^2=\\mathrm{BE}_{\\text {products }}-\\mathrm{BE}_{\\text {reactants }} $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\Delta \\mathrm{Mc}^2=61.0 \\mathrm{MeV}-63.2 \\mathrm{MeV}=-2.2 \\mathrm{MeV} $$\n\nThe negative sign indicates that the process is endothermic, meaning energy must be supplied for the reaction to occur because the product is less stable than the reactants.\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the energy change in the process is 2.2 MeV .\n\nThus, the correct option is (C).",
      "source": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Morning Shift | Nuclear Physics | mnswhqw8",
      "pyq": true,
      "exactPaperText": true,
      "pyqSourceSlug": "atoms-and-nuclei"
