      "a": 1,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 5th April Evening Shift. Correct option: B. Mass defect $(\\Delta \\mathrm{m})$ is the difference between the sum of the masses of the individual nucleons (protons and neutrons) and the actual experimental mass of the nucleus.\n\nFor the atom $\\mathrm{C}_6^{12}$, atomic number is $\\mathrm{Z}=6$ (Number of protons)\n\nMass number is $\\mathrm{A}=12$\n\nNumber of neutrons $\\mathrm{N}=\\mathrm{A}-\\mathrm{Z}=12-6=6$\n\nSo, mass of 6 protons $=6 \\times 1.00727 u=6.04362 u$\n\nAnd the mass of 6 neutrons $=6 \\times 1.00866 u=6.05196 u$\n\nSo, total theoretical mass $=6.04362+6.05196=12.09558 \\mathrm{u}$\n\nIt is given that the experimental mass of $\\mathrm{C}_6^{12}=12 \\mathrm{u}$ :\n\nHence, the mass defect is\n\n$$ \\Delta \\mathrm{m}=\\text { (Total theoretical mass) }- \\text { (Experimental mass) } $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\Delta \\mathrm{m}=12.09558 \\mathrm{u}-12 \\mathrm{u}=0.09558 \\mathrm{u} $$\n\nThe energy equivalent of 1 u is given as $931.5 \\mathrm{MeV} / \\mathrm{c}^2$.\n\n$$ \\Delta \\mathrm{m}\\left(\\text { in } \\mathrm{MeV} / \\mathrm{c}^2\\right)=0.09558 \\times 931.5 $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$$$ \\Delta \\mathrm{m} \\approx 89.03277 \\approx 89.03 \\mathrm{MeV} / \\mathrm{c}^{\\wedge} 2 $$\n\nTherefore, the mass defect of the $\\mathrm{C}_6^{12}$ atom is approximately $89.03 \\mathrm{MeV} / \\mathrm{c}^2$.\n\nThus, the correct option is (B).",
      "source": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 5th April Evening Shift | Nuclear Physics | mnswhvcf",
      "pyq": true,
      "exactPaperText": true,
      "pyqSourceSlug": "atoms-and-nuclei"
