      "q": "Which of the following contain the same number of atoms?\n\n(Given : Molar mass in $\\mathrm{g} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ of $\\mathrm{H}, \\mathrm{He}, \\mathrm{O}$ and S are 1, 4, 16 and 32 respectively)\n\nA. 2 g of $\\mathrm{O}_2$ gas\n\nB. 4 g of $\\mathrm{SO}_2$ gas\n\nC. 1400 mL of $\\mathrm{O}_2$ at STP\n\nD. $0.05 \\mathrm{~L}$ of He at STP\n\nE. $ 0.0625 \\mathrm{~mol}$ of $\\mathrm{H}_2$ gas\n\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :",
      "qHtml": "<p>Which of the following contain the same number of atoms?</p>\n<p>(Given : Molar mass in $\\mathrm{g} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ of $\\mathrm{H}, \\mathrm{He}, \\mathrm{O}$ and S are 1, 4, 16 and 32 respectively)</p>\n<p>A. 2 g of $\\mathrm{O}_2$ gas</p>\n<p>B. 4 g of $\\mathrm{SO}_2$ gas</p>\n<p>C. 1400 mL of $\\mathrm{O}_2$ at STP</p>\n<p>D. $0.05 \\mathrm{~L}$ of He at STP</p>\n<p>E. $ 0.0625 \\mathrm{~mol}$ of $\\mathrm{H}_2$ gas</p>\n<p>Choose the correct answer from the options given below :</p>",
      "o": [
        "A and B only",
        "B and C only",
        "C and D only",
        "A, C and E only"
      ],
      "oHtml": [
        "<p>A and B only</p>",
        "<p>B and C only</p>",
        "<p>C and D only</p>",
        "<p>A, C and E only</p>"
      ],
      "a": 3,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 6th April Evening Shift. Correct option: D. $$ \\text{Total number of atoms} = (\\text{Number of moles}) \\times N_A \\times (\\text{Atomicity}) $$\n\nLet's calculate this for each option.\n\nA. 2 g of $\\mathrm{O}_2$ gas\n\nMolar mass of $\\mathrm{O}_2$: The atomic mass of O is 16 u. So, the molar mass of $\\mathrm{O}_2$ is $2 \\times 16 = 32 \\ \\text{g mol}^{-1}$.\n\nNumber of moles ($n$):\n\n$$ n = \\frac{\\text{Given mass}}{\\text{Molar mass}} = \\frac{2 \\ \\text{g}}{32 \\ \\text{g mol}^{-1}} = \\frac{1}{16} \\ \\text{mol} $$\n\nAtomicity of $\\mathrm{O}_2$: One molecule of $\\mathrm{O}_2$ contains 2 oxygen atoms. So, the atomicity is 2.\n\nTotal number of atoms:\n\n$$ \\text{Atoms} = n \\times N_A \\times \\text{Atomicity} = \\frac{1}{16} \\times N_A \\times 2 = \\frac{2}{16} N_A = \\frac{1}{8} N_A $$\n\nB. 4 g of $\\mathrm{SO}_2$ gas\n\nMolar mass of $\\mathrm{SO}_2$: Atomic mass of S is 32 u and O is 16 u. So, the molar mass of $\\mathrm{SO}_2$ is $32 + (2 \\times 16) = 32 + 32 = 64 \\ \\text{g mol}^{-1}$.\n\nNumber of moles ($n$):\n\n$$ n = \\frac{\\text{Given mass}}{\\text{Molar mass}} = \\frac{4 \\ \\text{g}}{64 \\ \\text{g mol}^{-1}} = \\frac{1}{16} \\ \\text{mol} $$\n\nAtomicity of $\\mathrm{SO}_2$: One molecule of $\\mathrm{SO}_2$ contains 1 sulfur atom and 2 oxygen atoms, making a total of $1+2=3$ atoms. The atomicity is 3.\n\nTotal number of atoms:\n\n$$ \\text{Atoms} = n \\times N_A \\times \\text{Atomicity} = \\frac{1}{16} \\times N_A \\times 3 = \\frac{3}{16} N_A $$\n\nC. 1400 mL of $\\mathrm{O}_2$ at STP\n\nMolar volume at STP: We know that 1 mole of any gas at STP occupies 22400 mL (or 22.4 L).\n\nNumber of moles ($n$):\n\n$$ n = \\frac{\\text{Given volume}}{\\text{Molar volume at STP}} = \\frac{1400 \\ \\text{mL}}{22400 \\ \\text{mL mol}^{-1}} = \\frac{14}{224} = \\frac{1}{16} \\ \\text{mol} $$\n\nAtomicity of $\\mathrm{O}_2$: As we found earlier, the atomicity is 2.\n\nTotal number of atoms:\n\n$$ \\text{Atoms} = n \\times N_A \\times \\text{Atomicity} = \\frac{1}{16} \\times N_A \\times 2 = \\frac{2}{16} N_A = \\frac{1}{8} N_A $$\n\nD. 0.05 L of He at STP\n\nMolar volume at STP: 1 mole of any gas at STP occupies 22.4 L.\n\nNumber of moles ($n$):\n\n$$ n = \\frac{\\text{Given volume}}{\\text{Molar volume at STP}} = \\frac{0.05 \\ \\text{L}}{22.4 \\ \\text{L mol}^{-1}} \\approx 0.0022 \\ \\text{mol} $$\n\nAtomicity of He: Helium (He) is a noble gas and exists as single atoms. So, its atomicity is 1.\n\nTotal number of atoms:\n\n$$ \\text{Atoms} = n \\times N_A \\times \\text{Atomicity} = \\frac{0.05}{22.4} \\times N_A \\times 1 = \\frac{0.05}{22.4} N_A $$\n\nE. 0.0625 mol of $\\mathrm{H}_2$ gas\n\nNumber of moles ($n$): This is given directly as $0.0625 \\ \\text{mol}$. Let's convert this decimal to a fraction to make comparison easier: $0.0625 = \\frac{625}{10000} = \\frac{1}{16}$. So, $n = \\frac{1}{16} \\ \\text{mol}$.\n\nAtomicity of $\\mathrm{H}_2$: One molecule of $\\mathrm{H}_2$ contains 2 hydrogen atoms. The atomicity is 2.\n\nTotal number of atoms:\n\n$$ \\text{Atoms} = n \\times N_A \\times \\text{Atomicity} = \\frac{1}{16} \\times N_A \\times 2 = \\frac{2}{16} N_A = \\frac{1}{8} N_A $$\n\nConclusion\n\nLet's summarise the total number of atoms we calculated for each option:\n\nA: $\\frac{1}{8} N_A$ atoms\n\nB: $\\frac{3}{16} N_A$ atoms\n\nC: $\\frac{1}{8} N_A$ atoms\n\nD: $\\frac{0.05}{22.4} N_A$ atoms\n\nE: $\\frac{1}{8} N_A$ atoms\n\nBy comparing these results, we can clearly see that options A, C, and E contain the same number of atoms.\n\nTherefore, the correct choice is the one that lists A, C, and E.\n\nThe correct answer from the options given below is Option D .",
      "source": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 6th April Evening Shift | Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry | mnsik8q5",
