      "q": "The species having identical radii according to the Bohr's theory are :\n\nA. H (first orbit)\n\nB. $\\mathrm{He}^{+}$(first orbit)\n\nC. $\\mathrm{He}^{+}$(Second orbit)\n\nD. $\\mathrm{Li}^{2+}$ (first orbit)\n\nE. $\\mathrm{Be}^{3+}$ (Second orbit)\n\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :",
      "qHtml": "<p>The species having identical radii according to the Bohr's theory are :</p>\n<p>A. H (first orbit)</p>\n<p>B. $\\mathrm{He}^{+}$(first orbit)</p>\n<p>C. $\\mathrm{He}^{+}$(Second orbit)</p>\n<p>D. $\\mathrm{Li}^{2+}$ (first orbit)</p>\n<p>E. $\\mathrm{Be}^{3+}$ (Second orbit)</p>\n<p>Choose the correct answer from the options given below :</p>",
      "o": [
        "A and C Only",
        "A and E Only",
        "B and E Only",
        "C and D Only"
      ],
      "oHtml": [
        "<p>A and C Only</p>",
        "<p>A and E Only</p>",
        "<p>B and E Only</p>",
        "<p>C and D Only</p>"
      ],
      "a": 1,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Evening Shift. Correct option: B. For a hydrogen-like species, according to Bohr’s theory, the radius of the $n^{\\text{th}}$ orbit is\n\n$$ r_n = \\frac{n^2 a_0}{Z} $$\n\nwhere:\n\n$n$ = orbit number\n\n$Z$ = atomic number\n\n$a_0$ = Bohr radius\n\nSo, for identical radii, the value of $\\dfrac{n^2}{Z}$ must be same.\n\nNow check each species:\n\nA. H (first orbit)\n\nFor hydrogen, $Z=1$, $n=1$\n\n$$ r = \\frac{1^2 a_0}{1} = a_0 $$\n\nB. $\\mathrm{He}^{+}$ (first orbit)\n\nHere, $Z=2$, $n=1$\n\n$$ r = \\frac{1^2 a_0}{2} = \\frac{a_0}{2} $$\n\nC. $\\mathrm{He}^{+}$ (second orbit)\n\nHere, $Z=2$, $n=2$\n\n$$ r = \\frac{2^2 a_0}{2} = \\frac{4a_0}{2} = 2a_0 $$\n\nD. $\\mathrm{Li}^{2+}$ (first orbit)\n\nHere, $Z=3$, $n=1$\n\n$$ r = \\frac{1^2 a_0}{3} = \\frac{a_0}{3} $$\n\nE. $\\mathrm{Be}^{3+}$ (second orbit)\n\nHere, $Z=4$, $n=2$\n\n$$ r = \\frac{2^2 a_0}{4} = \\frac{4a_0}{4} = a_0 $$\n\nNow compare:\n\nA: $a_0$\n\nB: $\\dfrac{a_0}{2}$\n\nC: $2a_0$\n\nD: $\\dfrac{a_0}{3}$\n\nE: $a_0$\n\nSo, the identical radii are for A and E .\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is:\n\n$$ \\boxed{\\text{Option B: A and E Only}} $$",
      "source": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Evening Shift | Structure of Atom | mnswhswn",
