      "s": "JEE Main 2023 (Online) 6th April Evening Shift. Correct option: D. The melting point of gallium is very low (about 29.76°C or 85.57°F). Therefore, gallium can melt just from the heat of one's hand. The boiling point of gallium, on the other hand, is quite high (2400°C or 4352°F).\n\nOn the contrary, water boils at 100°C (212°F) under normal atmospheric pressure. Therefore, inside boiling water, gallium would melt into a liquid but would not boil or evaporate, unlike the other elements listed. Lithium (Li), Bromine (Br), and Cesium (Cs) have boiling points below 100°C, so they would turn into a gas in boiling water.",
