      "a": 1,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Evening Shift. Correct option: B. For neutralization of acetic acid:\n\n$$ \\mathrm{CH_3COOH + NaOH \\rightarrow CH_3COONa + H_2O} $$\n\nThe reaction is in the ratio $1:1$.\n\nGiven that $20\\ \\text{mL}$ of acetic acid requires $28.4\\ \\text{mL}$ of $0.1\\ \\text{M}$ NaOH for complete neutralization.\n\nSo, moles of acetic acid in $20\\ \\text{mL}$ are:\n\n$$ n(\\mathrm{CH_3COOH}) = 0.1 \\times \\frac{28.4}{1000} = 2.84 \\times 10^{-3}\\ \\text{mol} $$\n\nNow solution $X$ is prepared by mixing:\n\n$20\\ \\text{mL}$ acetic acid\n\n$14.2\\ \\text{mL}$ of $0.1\\ \\text{M}$ NaOH\n\nMoles of NaOH added:\n\n$$ n(\\mathrm{NaOH}) = 0.1 \\times \\frac{14.2}{1000} = 1.42 \\times 10^{-3}\\ \\text{mol} $$\n\nNaOH will neutralize equal moles of acetic acid.\n\nInitial moles of acetic acid:\n\n$$ 2.84 \\times 10^{-3} $$\n\nMoles neutralized:\n\n$$ 1.42 \\times 10^{-3} $$\n\nMoles of acetic acid left:\n\n$$ 2.84 \\times 10^{-3} - 1.42 \\times 10^{-3} = 1.42 \\times 10^{-3} $$\n\nMoles of acetate formed:\n\n$$ 1.42 \\times 10^{-3} $$\n\nSo after mixing, we have:\n\nacetic acid = $1.42 \\times 10^{-3}\\ \\text{mol}$\n\nacetate ion = $1.42 \\times 10^{-3}\\ \\text{mol}$\n\nThis is a buffer solution where:\n\n$$ [\\text{salt}] = [\\text{acid}] $$\n\nUsing Henderson–Hasselbalch equation:\n\n$$ \\mathrm{pH} = \\mathrm{p}K_a + \\log \\frac{[\\text{salt}]}{[\\text{acid}]} $$\n\nSince both are equal,\n\n$$ \\frac{[\\text{salt}]}{[\\text{acid}]} = 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\log 1 = 0 $$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$ \\mathrm{pH} = \\mathrm{p}K_a = 4.75 $$\n\nSo, the correct answer is:\n\n$$ \\boxed{4.75} $$\n\nOption B.",
