      "a": 0,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Morning Shift. Correct option: A. The oxidising power of halogens decreases in the order $F_2 > Cl_2 > Br_2 > I_2$. This means that fluorine is the strongest oxidising agent and iodine is the weakest. Therefore, $Cl_2$ can easily oxidise bromide and iodide ions to form $Br_2$ and $I_2$ respectively. This principle is used in the “Layer test”.\n\nIn this test, chlorine gas is passed through an aqueous solution containing bromide or iodide ions. A redox (oxidation-reduction) reaction takes place, where chlorine oxidises bromide or iodide ions. The chemical equations are as follows:\n\n$$\\mathrm{NaBr} + \\mathrm{Cl}_2 \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Br}_2 + \\mathrm{NaCl}$$\n\n$$\\mathrm{NaI} + \\mathrm{Cl}_2 \\rightarrow \\mathrm{I}_2 + \\mathrm{NaCl}$$\n\nSo, $Cl_2$ displaces $Br_2$ and $I_2$ from their compounds since chlorine has higher oxidising power. This confirms that both statements (I) and (II) are true, and statement (II) correctly explains statement (I).",
      "source": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Morning Shift | Redox Reactions | mnrlqhm4",
      "pyq": true,
      "exactPaperText": true,
      "pyqSourceSlug": "redox-reactions"
