      "a": 0,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 8th April Evening Shift. Correct option: A. Molar masses:\n\nC = 12 g mol⁻¹\n\nH = 1 g mol⁻¹\n\nO = 16 g mol⁻¹\n\nCl = 35.5 g mol⁻¹\n\nFormula masses:\n\nFor methanol, CH₃OH:\n\n$ M = 12 + (3 \\times 1) + 16 + 1 = 32\\ \\text{g mol}^{-1} $\n\nFor carbon tetrachloride, CCl₄:\n\n$ M = 12 + (4 \\times 35.5) = 12 + 142 = 154\\ \\text{g mol}^{-1} $\n\nThis means in 100 g of solution :\n\nMass of methanol = 30 g\n\nMass of CCl₄ = 70 g\n\n$ n_{\\text{methanol}} = \\frac{30}{32} = 0.9375\\ \\text{mol} $\n\n$ n_{\\text{CCl}_4} = \\frac{70}{154} = 0.4545\\ \\text{mol} $\n\nTotal moles = $ 0.9375 + 0.4545 = 1.392 $\n\nSo,\n\n$ x_{\\text{methanol}} = \\frac{0.9375}{1.392} = 0.674 $\n\n$ x_{\\text{CCl}_4} = \\frac{0.4545}{1.392} = 0.326 \\approx 0.33 $\n\nHence, Statement I is correct.\n\nMethanol is polar and forms hydrogen bonds.\n\nCarbon tetrachloride is non-polar.\n\nWhen they are mixed, the H-bonding between methanol molecules weakens (since CCl₄ cannot form hydrogen bonds with methanol).\n\n→ The intermolecular attraction between unlike molecules decreases.\n\nAs a result, the escaping tendency of molecules increases and vapour pressure increases , leading to positive deviation from Raoult’s law.\n\nHence, Statement II is also correct.\n\nFinal Answer\n\nBoth Statement I and Statement II are true.\n\n$ \\boxed{\\text{Option A}} $",
