      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 5th April Evening Shift. Correct option: D. The correct answer is Option D: $ \\mathrm{K}_2 \\mathrm{Cr}_2 \\mathrm{O}_7 $ .\n\nA paper dipped in dilute $ \\mathrm{H}_2\\mathrm{SO}_4 $ solution of $X$ turns green when treated with $ \\mathrm{SO}_2 $ gas.\n\nThis means that $X$ must be a substance which changes to green on reduction by $ \\mathrm{SO}_2 $.\n\nNow, $ \\mathrm{SO}_2 $ is a reducing agent .\n\nAcidified potassium dichromate, that is, $ \\mathrm{K}_2\\mathrm{Cr}_2\\mathrm{O}_7 $ in dilute $ \\mathrm{H}_2\\mathrm{SO}_4 $, is orange in colour and gets reduced to $ \\mathrm{Cr}^{3+} $, which is green .\n\nThe reaction is:\n\n$$ \\mathrm{Cr}_2\\mathrm{O}_7^{2-} + 3\\mathrm{SO}_2 + 2\\mathrm{H}^+ \\rightarrow 2\\mathrm{Cr}^{3+} + 3\\mathrm{SO}_4^{2-} + \\mathrm{H}_2\\mathrm{O} $$\n\nSo, the orange dichromate paper turns green.\n\nNow check other options briefly:\n\nA: $ \\mathrm{KI}$-starch\n\nThis is used to detect oxidising agents; it gives blue colour due to iodine-starch complex, not green.\n\nB: $ \\mathrm{KMnO}_4 $\n\nAcidified $ \\mathrm{KMnO}_4 $ gets decolourised by $ \\mathrm{SO}_2 $, not green.\n\nC: $ \\mathrm{Pb}(\\mathrm{CH}_3\\mathrm{COO})_2 $\n\nLead acetate paper is used for detecting $ \\mathrm{H}_2\\mathrm{S} $, giving black colour.\n\nHence, the compound $X$ is\n\n$$ \\boxed{\\mathrm{K}_2\\mathrm{Cr}_2\\mathrm{O}_7} $$",
