      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Morning Shift. Correct option: B. We need to solve\n\n$$ |x^2+x-9|=|x|+|x^2-9|. $$\n\nWe will do it by considering intervals where the expressions inside modulus change sign.\n\nThe critical points are obtained from:\n\n$x=0$ from $|x|$\n\n$x=\\pm 3$ from $|x^2-9|$\n\nroots of $x^2+x-9=0$, but for NCERT-style interval splitting here, it is enough to use the main sign-change points $-3,0,3$ and then check sign of $x^2+x-9$ in each interval.\n\nNow solve interval-wise.\n\n1. For $x\\le -3$\n\nHere,\n\n$|x|=-x$\n\n$|x^2-9|=x^2-9$\n\nAlso $x^2+x-9>0$, so $|x^2+x-9|=x^2+x-9$\n\nThus,\n\n$$ x^2+x-9=-x+(x^2-9) $$\n\n$$ x^2+x-9=x^2-x-9 $$\n\n$$ x=-x $$\n\n$$ 2x=0 \\Rightarrow x=0 $$\n\nBut $0\\not\\le -3$, so no solution here.\n\n2. For $-3\\le x\\le 0$\n\nHere,\n\n$|x|=-x$\n\n$|x^2-9|=9-x^2$\n\nNow we check $x^2+x-9$ on this interval.\n\nAt $x=-3$,\n\n$$ x^2+x-9=9-3-9=-3 At $x=0$,\n\n$$ x^2+x-9=-9 So throughout this interval,\n\n$$ |x^2+x-9|=-(x^2+x-9)=-x^2-x+9. $$\n\nHence equation becomes\n\n$$ -x^2-x+9=-x+(9-x^2). $$\n\nSimplify RHS:\n\n$$ -x^2-x+9=-x+9-x^2. $$\n\nBoth sides are identical, so this is true for every $x\\in[-3,0]$.\n\nSo all $x\\in[-3,0]$ are solutions.\n\n3. For $0\\le x\\le 3$\n\nHere,\n\n$|x|=x$\n\n$|x^2-9|=9-x^2$\n\nAlso on this interval,\n\n$$ x^2+x-9 $$\n\nAt $x=0$, it is $-9 0$.\n\nSo the sign changes inside this interval.\n\nLet us find the root:\n\n$$ x^2+x-9=0 $$\n\n$$ x=\\frac{-1\\pm\\sqrt{37}}{2}. $$\n\nThe positive root is\n\n$$ \\frac{-1+\\sqrt{37}}{2}. $$\n\nSo split further.\n\n(i) For $0\\le x\\le \\frac{-1+\\sqrt{37}}{2}$\n\nHere $x^2+x-9\\le 0$, so\n\n$$ |x^2+x-9|=-x^2-x+9. $$\n\nEquation:\n\n$$ -x^2-x+9=x+(9-x^2) $$\n\n$$ -x^2-x+9=-x^2+x+9 $$\n\n$$ -x=x $$\n\n$$ 2x=0 \\Rightarrow x=0. $$\n\nSo only $x=0$, already included in previous interval.\n\n(ii) For $\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{37}}{2}\\le x\\le 3$\n\nHere $x^2+x-9\\ge 0$, so\n\n$$ |x^2+x-9|=x^2+x-9. $$\n\nEquation:\n\n$$ x^2+x-9=x+(9-x^2) $$\n\n$$ x^2-9=9-x^2 $$\n\n$$ 2x^2=18 $$\n\n$$ x^2=9 $$\n\n$$ x=\\pm 3. $$\n\nIn this interval, only $x=3$ is valid.\n\nSo from $[0,3]$, only $x=0,3$ are solutions.\n\n4. For $x\\ge 3$\n\nHere,\n\n$|x|=x$\n\n$|x^2-9|=x^2-9$\n\n$x^2+x-9>0$, so $|x^2+x-9|=x^2+x-9$\n\nThus,\n\n$$ x^2+x-9=x+(x^2-9) $$\n\n$$ x^2+x-9=x^2+x-9 $$\n\nThis is true for every $x\\ge 3$.\n\nSo all $x\\in[3,\\infty)$ are solutions.\n\nComplete solution set\n\nCombining all intervals:\n\n$$ [-3,0]\\cup[3,\\infty). $$\n\nThus,\n\n$$ \\alpha=-3,\\quad \\beta=0,\\quad \\gamma=3. $$\n\nNow,\n\n$$ \\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2=(-3)^2+0^2+3^2=9+0+9=18. $$\n\nSo the correct answer is\n\n$$ \\boxed{18} $$\n\nHence, Option B is correct.",
