      "a": 0,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Evening Shift. Correct option: A. For\n\n$$ f:[1,\\infty)\\to [1,\\infty),\\qquad f(x)=(x-1)^4+1, $$\n\nwe first find the inverse function.\n\nSince for $x\\ge 1$, the function $(x-1)^4+1$ is strictly increasing, $f$ is one-one and onto from $[1,\\infty)$ to $[1,\\infty)$.\n\nLet\n\n$$ y=(x-1)^4+1. $$\n\nThen\n\n$$ y-1=(x-1)^4. $$\n\nSince $x\\ge 1$, we take the positive fourth root:\n\n$$ x-1=(y-1)^{1/4}. $$\n\nHence\n\n$$ f^{-1}(y)=1+(y-1)^{1/4}. $$\n\nSo\n\n$$ f^{-1}(x)=1+(x-1)^{1/4}. $$\n\nNow check the two statements one by one.\n\nStatement (I)\n\nWe need to solve\n\n$$ f(x)=f^{-1}(x). $$\n\nThat is,\n\n$$ (x-1)^4+1=1+(x-1)^{1/4}. $$\n\nSo\n\n$$ (x-1)^4=(x-1)^{1/4}. $$\n\nPut\n\n$$ t=x-1 \\quad \\text{where } t\\ge 0. $$\n\nThen the equation becomes\n\n$$ t^4=t^{1/4}. $$\n\nNow solve:\n\n$$ t^4-t^{1/4}=0. $$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$ t^{1/4}\\left(t^{15/4}-1\\right)=0. $$\n\nHence\n\n$$ t=0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad t^{15/4}=1. $$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0$, from $t^{15/4}=1$ we get\n\n$$ t=1. $$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$ x-1=0 \\Rightarrow x=1, $$\n\nor\n\n$$ x-1=1 \\Rightarrow x=2. $$\n\nSo the set\n\n$$ S=\\{x\\in[1,\\infty): f(x)=f^{-1}(x)\\} $$\n\ncontains exactly two elements:\n\n$$ S=\\{1,2\\}. $$\n\nThus, Statement (I) is TRUE.\n\nStatement (II)\n\nWe need to solve\n\n$$ f(x)=f^{-1}(x+1). $$\n\nNow\n\n$$ f(x)=(x-1)^4+1, $$\n\nand\n\n$$ f^{-1}(x+1)=1+\\big((x+1)-1\\big)^{1/4}=1+x^{1/4}. $$\n\nSo the equation becomes\n\n$$ (x-1)^4+1=1+x^{1/4}. $$\n\nHence\n\n$$ (x-1)^4=x^{1/4}. $$\n\nAgain put\n\n$$ t=x^{1/4}, \\quad t\\ge 1 \\text{ since } x\\ge 1. $$\n\nThen\n\n$$ x=t^4. $$\n\nSo the equation becomes\n\n$$ (t^4-1)^4=t. $$\n\nInstead of solving this directly, test simple values.\n\nFor $x=1$:\n\n$$ f(1)=(1-1)^4+1=1, $$\n\nand\n\n$$ f^{-1}(1+1)=f^{-1}(2)=1+(2-1)^{1/4}=2. $$\n\nSo $x=1$ is not a solution.\n\nFor $x=2$:\n\n$$ f(2)=(2-1)^4+1=2, $$\n\nand\n\n$$ f^{-1}(2+1)=f^{-1}(3)=1+(3-1)^{1/4}=1+2^{1/4}\\neq 2. $$\n\nSo $x=2$ is not a solution.\n\nNow define\n\n$$ g(x)=(x-1)^4-x^{1/4}, \\qquad x\\ge 1. $$\n\nWe need to check whether $g(x)=0$ has any solution.\n\nObserve:\n\n$$ g(1)=0-1=-1 and\n\n$$ g(2)=1-2^{1/4} while\n\n$$ g(3)=16-3^{1/4}>0. $$\n\nSince $g$ is continuous, there is at least one root in $(2,3)$.\n\nSo the set\n\n$$ S=\\{x\\in[1,\\infty): f(x)=f^{-1}(x+1)\\} $$\n\nis not empty.\n\nThus, Statement (II) is FALSE.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is\n\n$$ \\boxed{\\text{Option A: only (I) is TRUE}} $$",
