      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 6th April Evening Shift. Correct option: A. Let us solve\n\n$$ \\sin\\left(\\tan^{-1}(x\\sqrt{2})\\right)=\\cot\\left(\\sin^{-1}\\sqrt{1-x^2}\\right), \\qquad x\\in(0,1) $$\n\nstep by step.\n\nStep 1: Simplify the left-hand side\n\nLet\n\n$$ \\theta=\\tan^{-1}(x\\sqrt{2}) $$\n\nThen\n\n$$ \\tan\\theta=x\\sqrt{2} $$\n\nWe know that if $\\tan\\theta=\\dfrac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}}$, then we can take\n\nopposite $=x\\sqrt{2}$\n\nadjacent $=1$\n\nSo hypotenuse is\n\n$$ \\sqrt{1+(x\\sqrt{2})^2}=\\sqrt{1+2x^2} $$\n\nHence,\n\n$$ \\sin\\theta=\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{1+2x^2}} $$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$ \\sin\\left(\\tan^{-1}(x\\sqrt{2})\\right)=\\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{1+2x^2}} $$\n\nStep 2: Simplify the right-hand side\n\nLet\n\n$$ \\phi=\\sin^{-1}\\sqrt{1-x^2} $$\n\nThen\n\n$$ \\sin\\phi=\\sqrt{1-x^2} $$\n\nSince $x\\in(0,1)$, we have $\\sqrt{1-x^2}\\in(0,1)$, so $\\phi$ is acute.\n\nNow,\n\n$$ \\cos\\phi=\\sqrt{1-\\sin^2\\phi} = \\sqrt{1-(1-x^2)} = \\sqrt{x^2} = x $$\n\nbecause $x>0$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$ \\cot\\phi=\\frac{\\cos\\phi}{\\sin\\phi} =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}} $$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ \\cot\\left(\\sin^{-1}\\sqrt{1-x^2}\\right)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}} $$\n\nStep 3: Equate both sides\n\nNow the given equation becomes\n\n$$ \\frac{x\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{1+2x^2}}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}} $$\n\nSince $x\\in(0,1)$, we have $x\\neq 0$, so divide both sides by $x$:\n\n$$ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{1+2x^2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}} $$\n\nStep 4: Cross multiply\n\n$$ \\sqrt{2}\\,\\sqrt{1-x^2}=\\sqrt{1+2x^2} $$\n\nNow square both sides:\n\n$$ 2(1-x^2)=1+2x^2 $$\n\n$$ 2-2x^2=1+2x^2 $$\n\n$$ 1=4x^2 $$\n\n$$ x^2=\\frac{1}{4} $$\n\nSince $x\\in(0,1)$,\n\n$$ x=\\frac{1}{2} $$\n\nFinal Answer\n\n$$ \\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} $$\n\nSo, the correct option is Option A .",
