      "a": 1,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Morning Shift. Correct option: B. Coefficient of $x^3$ in the given expression\n\n$$ (1+x)^3+(1+x)^4+\\cdots +(1+x)^{99}+(1+kx)^{100} $$\n\nis obtained by adding the coefficients of $x^3$ from each term.\n\nFor $(1+x)^r$, coefficient of $x^3$ is\n\n$$ {}^rC_3 $$\n\nSo, from\n\n$$ (1+x)^3+(1+x)^4+\\cdots +(1+x)^{99} $$\n\nthe total coefficient of $x^3$ is\n\n$$ \\sum_{r=3}^{99} {}^rC_3 $$\n\nAlso, in $(1+kx)^{100}$, coefficient of $x^3$ is\n\n$$ {}^{100}C_3\\,k^3 $$\n\nHence total coefficient of $x^3$ is\n\n$$ \\sum_{r=3}^{99} {}^rC_3 + {}^{100}C_3 k^3 $$\n\nNow use the standard NCERT identity:\n\n$$ \\sum_{r=3}^{99} {}^rC_3 = {}^{100}C_4 $$\n\nSo coefficient becomes\n\n$$ {}^{100}C_4 + {}^{100}C_3 k^3 $$\n\nGiven that this equals\n\n$$ \\left(43n+\\frac{101}{4}\\right){}^{100}C_3 $$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$ {}^{100}C_4 + {}^{100}C_3 k^3 = \\left(43n+\\frac{101}{4}\\right){}^{100}C_3 $$\n\nDivide by ${}^{100}C_3$:\n\n$$ \\frac{{}^{100}C_4}{{}^{100}C_3}+k^3 = 43n+\\frac{101}{4} $$\n\nNow,\n\n$$ \\frac{{}^{100}C_4}{{}^{100}C_3} = \\frac{100-3}{4} = \\frac{97}{4} $$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ k^3+\\frac{97}{4}=43n+\\frac{101}{4} $$\n\nThus,\n\n$$ k^3=43n+1 $$\n\nWe need the smallest $k\\in\\mathbb N$ such that\n\n$$ k^3-1 $$\n\nis divisible by $43$.\n\nThat is,\n\n$$ k^3\\equiv 1 \\pmod{43} $$\n\nNow check smallest natural numbers:\n\n$$ 1^3=1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{43} $$\n\nSo the smallest value is\n\n$$ k=1 $$\n\nHence,\n\n$$ 1=43n+1 $$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$ n=0 $$\n\nBut in the question, $n\\in \\mathbb N$. In school-level convention, if $\\mathbb N=\\{0,1,2,\\dots\\}$, then this is allowed.\n\nThus,\n\n$$ p=1,\\quad n=0 $$\n\nand\n\n$$ p+n=1 $$\n\nBut this is not among the options, so clearly the intended convention is $n\\ge 1$.\n\nNow take the smallest $n\\in\\mathbb N$ with $n\\ge 1$ such that\n\n$$ k^3=43n+1 $$\n\nTry $n=1$:\n\n$$ k^3=44 \\quad \\text{not a cube} $$\n\n$n=2$:\n\n$$ k^3=87 \\quad \\text{not a cube} $$\n\n$n=3$:\n\n$$ k^3=130 \\quad \\text{not a cube} $$\n\n$n=4$:\n\n$$ k^3=173 $$\n\n$n=5$:\n\n$$ k^3=216=6^3 $$\n\nSo smallest such $k$ is\n\n$$ p=6,\\quad n=5 $$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$ p+n=6+5=11 $$\n\nHence the correct answer is:\n\n$$ \\boxed{11} $$\n\nSo, Option B is correct.",
