      "a": 1,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 5th April Evening Shift. Correct option: B. Vertex of the parabola\n\n$$ y=x^2-6x+12 $$\n\nis found by completing the square:\n\n$$ y=(x-3)^2+3 $$\n\nSo, the vertex is\n\n$$ P=(3,3) $$\n\nNow the circle is\n\n$$ x^2+y^2-2x-4y+3=0 $$\n\nComplete squares:\n\n$$ (x-1)^2+(y-2)^2=2 $$\n\nHence, the centre of the circle is\n\n$$ C=(1,2) $$\n\nand radius is\n\n$$ r=\\sqrt{2} $$\n\nNow,\n\n$$ PC=\\sqrt{(3-1)^2+(3-2)^2}=\\sqrt{4+1}=\\sqrt{5} $$\n\nSince the line through $P$ cuts the circle at $R$ and $S$, the line must intersect the circle in a chord $RS$.\n\nLet the perpendicular distance from the centre $C$ to this line be $d$.\n\nThen chord length\n\n$$ RS=2\\sqrt{r^2-d^2}=2\\sqrt{2-d^2} $$\n\nAlso, since $P$ lies on the line outside the circle, and $R,S$ are the intersection points on opposite sides along the line, we have\n\n$$ PR+PS=RS $$\n\nonly if $P$ lies between them, which is not possible here because $P$ is outside the circle.\n\nSo let us use power of a point.\n\nIf $PR$ and $PS$ are the two secant segments from $P$, then\n\n$$ PR \\cdot PS = PC^2-r^2=5-2=3 $$\n\nLet the line make an angle such that the foot from $C$ to the line gives distance $d$.\n\nIf $M$ is the midpoint of chord $RS$, then\n\n$$ CM=d,\\qquad PM=\\sqrt{PC^2-d^2}=\\sqrt{5-d^2} $$\n\nbecause in right triangle with hypotenuse $PC$.\n\nAlso\n\n$$ MR=MS=\\sqrt{r^2-d^2}=\\sqrt{2-d^2} $$\n\nSince $P$ is outside the circle, along the secant line,\n\n$$ PR=PM-MR,\\qquad PS=PM+MR $$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$ PR+PS = 2PM = 2\\sqrt{5-d^2} $$\n\nSo\n\n$$ (PR+PS)^2=4(5-d^2) $$\n\nTo maximize this, we must minimize $d^2$.\n\nThe minimum possible value of $d$ is $0$, when the line passes through the centre $C$.\n\nHence maximum value is\n\n$$ (PR+PS)^2_{\\max}=4\\times 5=20 $$\n\nTherefore, the required maximum value is\n\n$$ \\boxed{20} $$\n\nSo the correct option is B .",
