      "a": 1,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 6th April Morning Shift. Correct option: B. Given,\n\n$$ \\cos \\theta + 1 = \\sqrt{3}\\sin \\theta $$\n\nWe have to find all $\\theta \\in (-2\\pi, 2\\pi)$ satisfying this equation, and then calculate their sum.\n\nRewrite the equation as\n\n$$ 1+\\cos\\theta=\\sqrt{3}\\sin\\theta $$\n\nNow use the standard identities:\n\n$$ 1+\\cos\\theta = 2\\cos^2\\frac{\\theta}{2} $$\n\nand\n\n$$ \\sin\\theta = 2\\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\cos\\frac{\\theta}{2} $$\n\nSubstituting these,\n\n$$ 2\\cos^2\\frac{\\theta}{2}=\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 2\\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\cos\\frac{\\theta}{2} $$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ 2\\cos\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\left(\\cos\\frac{\\theta}{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right)=0 $$\n\nHence, either\n\nCase 1:\n\n$$ \\cos\\frac{\\theta}{2}=0 $$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ \\frac{\\theta}{2}=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi $$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$ \\theta=\\pi+2n\\pi $$\n\nNow in the interval $(-2\\pi,2\\pi)$, the possible values are:\n\n$$ \\theta=-\\pi,\\ \\pi $$\n\nCase 2:\n\n$$ \\cos\\frac{\\theta}{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2}=0 $$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ \\cos\\frac{\\theta}{2}=\\sqrt{3}\\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2} $$\n\nor\n\n$$ \\tan\\frac{\\theta}{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} $$\n\nThus,\n\n$$ \\frac{\\theta}{2}=\\frac{\\pi}{6}+n\\pi $$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ \\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{3}+2n\\pi $$\n\nNow in the interval $(-2\\pi,2\\pi)$, the possible values are:\n\n$$ \\theta=-\\frac{5\\pi}{3},\\ \\frac{\\pi}{3} $$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$ S=\\left\\{-\\pi,\\ \\pi,\\ -\\frac{5\\pi}{3},\\ \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right\\} $$\n\nNow sum these values:\n\n$$ -\\pi+\\pi-\\frac{5\\pi}{3}+\\frac{\\pi}{3} $$\n\n$$ = -\\frac{4\\pi}{3} $$\n\nHence,\n\n$$ \\sum_{\\theta\\in S}\\theta = -\\frac{4\\pi}{3} $$\n\nSo the correct answer is:\n\n$$ \\boxed{-\\frac{4\\pi}{3}} $$\n\nOption B",
