      "a": 1,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Morning Shift. Correct option: B. The given circle is\n\n$$ x^2+y^2+x-3y=0 $$\n\nand the point from which the two chords are drawn is\n\n$$ P(1,2) $$\n\nWe are told that these chords are bisected by the $y$-axis.\n\nLet the other end of one such chord be $R(x,y)$.\n\nSince the chord joining $P(1,2)$ and $R(x,y)$ is bisected by the $y$-axis, its midpoint must lie on the $y$-axis.\n\nSo the $x$-coordinate of the midpoint is $0$.\n\nMidpoint of $P(1,2)$ and $R(x,y)$ is\n\n$$ \\left(\\frac{1+x}{2}, \\frac{2+y}{2}\\right) $$\n\nSince this lies on the $y$-axis,\n\n$$ \\frac{1+x}{2}=0 $$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ x=-1 $$\n\nThus, the other end of each such chord must lie on the line\n\n$$ x=-1 $$\n\nNow find where this line cuts the circle.\n\nSubstitute $x=-1$ in\n\n$$ x^2+y^2+x-3y=0 $$\n\nWe get\n\n$$ (-1)^2+y^2+(-1)-3y=0 $$\n\n$$ 1+y^2-1-3y=0 $$\n\n$$ y^2-3y=0 $$\n\n$$ y(y-3)=0 $$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ y=0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y=3 $$\n\nHence the two points are\n\n$$ R(-1,0), \\quad S(-1,3) $$\n\nNow midpoint of $RS$ is\n\n$$ (\\alpha,\\beta)=\\left(\\frac{-1+(-1)}{2},\\frac{0+3}{2}\\right) $$\n\n$$ (\\alpha,\\beta)=\\left(-1,\\frac{3}{2}\\right) $$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$ \\alpha+\\beta=-1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} $$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ 6(\\alpha+\\beta)=6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=3 $$\n\nHence the correct answer is\n\n$$ \\boxed{3} $$\n\nSo, the correct option is Option B .",
