      "q": "Let for some $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}, f: \\mathbb{R} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ be a function satisfying $f(x+y)=f(x)+2 y^2+y+\\alpha x y$ for all $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}$. If $f(0)=-1$ and $f(1)=2$, then the value of $\\sum\\limits_{n=1}^5(\\alpha+f(n))$ is :",
      "qHtml": "<p>Let for some $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}, f: \\mathbb{R} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ be a function satisfying $f(x+y)=f(x)+2 y^2+y+\\alpha x y$ for all $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}$. If $f(0)=-1$ and $f(1)=2$, then the value of $\\sum\\limits_{n=1}^5(\\alpha+f(n))$ is :</p>",
