      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Morning Shift. Correct option: A. For the function\n\n$$ f(x)=\\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{4x+2[x]}{3}\\right), $$\n\nthe expression inside $\\cos^{-1}$ must lie between $-1$ and $1$.\n\nSo, for domain:\n\n$$ -1 \\le \\frac{4x+2[x]}{3} \\le 1 $$\n\nMultiplying by $3$:\n\n$$ -3 \\le 4x+2[x] \\le 3 $$\n\nNow let\n\n$$ [x]=n $$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer and\n\n$$ n \\le x Then the inequality becomes\n\n$$ -3 \\le 4x+2n \\le 3. $$\n\nSubtracting $2n$:\n\n$$ -3-2n \\le 4x \\le 3-2n $$\n\nDividing by $4$:\n\n$$ \\frac{-3-2n}{4} \\le x \\le \\frac{3-2n}{4} $$\n\nBut also,\n\n$$ n \\le x So we find possible integer values of $n$ for which these intervals overlap.\n\nCase 1: $n=-1$\n\nThen\n\n$$ [x]=-1,\\quad -1 \\le x and\n\n$$ \\frac{-3-2(-1)}{4} \\le x \\le \\frac{3-2(-1)}{4} $$\n\n$$ \\frac{-1}{4} \\le x \\le \\frac{5}{4} $$\n\nIntersection with $[-1,0)$ gives\n\n$$ -\\frac14 \\le x Case 2: $n=0$\n\nThen\n\n$$ 0 \\le x and\n\n$$ \\frac{-3}{4} \\le x \\le \\frac{3}{4} $$\n\nIntersection gives\n\n$$ 0 \\le x \\le \\frac34. $$\n\nCase 3: $n=-2$\n\nThen\n\n$$ -2 \\le x and\n\n$$ \\frac{-3-2(-2)}{4} \\le x \\le \\frac{3-2(-2)}{4} $$\n\n$$ \\frac14 \\le x \\le \\frac74 $$\n\nNo intersection.\n\nCase 4: $n=1$\n\nThen\n\n$$ 1 \\le x and\n\n$$ \\frac{-5}{4} \\le x \\le \\frac14 $$\n\nNo intersection.\n\nSo the domain is\n\n$$ \\left[-\\frac14,0\\right)\\cup\\left[0,\\frac34\\right] = \\left[-\\frac14,\\frac34\\right]. $$\n\nThus,\n\n$$ \\alpha=-\\frac14,\\quad \\beta=\\frac34. $$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$ \\alpha+\\beta=\\frac12 $$\n\nand\n\n$$ 12(\\alpha+\\beta)=12\\cdot \\frac12=6. $$\n\nHence the correct answer is\n\n$$ \\boxed{6} $$\n\nSo, Option A is correct.",
