      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 6th April Evening Shift. Correct option: B. To find the required quadratic equation, we must first find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$.\n\nGiven that there are 7 observations: $2, 4, \\alpha, 8, \\beta, 12, 14$. Let $n = 7$.\n\nThe mean of these observations ($\\bar{x}$) is $8$.\n\nAccording to the formula for the mean:\n\n$$ \\bar{x} = \\frac{\\sum x_i}{n} $$\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\n$$ 8 = \\frac{2 + 4 + \\alpha + 8 + \\beta + 12 + 14}{7} $$\n\n$$ 56 = 40 + \\alpha + \\beta $$\n\n$$ \\alpha + \\beta = 16 \\quad \\text{--- (1)} $$\n\nNext, the variance ($\\sigma^2$) is given as $16$. The formula for variance is:\n\n$$ \\sigma^2 = \\frac{\\sum x_i^2}{n} - (\\bar{x})^2 $$\n\nSubstituting the known values:\n\n$$ 16 = \\frac{2^2 + 4^2 + \\alpha^2 + 8^2 + \\beta^2 + 12^2 + 14^2}{7} - (8)^2 $$\n\n$$ 16 = \\frac{4 + 16 + \\alpha^2 + 64 + \\beta^2 + 144 + 196}{7} - 64 $$\n\n$$ 16 + 64 = \\frac{424 + \\alpha^2 + \\beta^2}{7} $$\n\n$$ 80 \\times 7 = 424 + \\alpha^2 + \\beta^2 $$\n\n$$ 560 = 424 + \\alpha^2 + \\beta^2 $$\n\n$$ \\alpha^2 + \\beta^2 = 136 \\quad \\text{--- (2)} $$\n\nNow, using the algebraic identity $(\\alpha + \\beta)^2 = \\alpha^2 + \\beta^2 + 2\\alpha \\beta $, we can find the product $\\alpha \\beta$:\n\n$$ (16)^2 = 136 + 2\\alpha \\beta $$\n\n$$ 256 = 136 + 2\\alpha \\beta $$\n\n$$ 2\\alpha \\beta = 120 $$\n\n$$ \\alpha \\beta = 60 $$\n\nThe numbers $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are the roots of the quadratic equation $t^2 - (\\alpha + \\beta)t + \\alpha \\beta = 0$:\n\n$$ t^2 - 16t + 60 = 0 $$\n\n$$ t^2 - 10t - 6t + 60 = 0 $$\n\n$$ t(t - 10) - 6(t - 10) = 0 $$\n\n$$ (t - 6)(t - 10) = 0 $$\n\nThus, the values are $6$ and $10$. Since we are given that $\\alpha $$ \\alpha = 6 $$\n\n$$ \\beta = 10 $$\n\nWe are asked to find the quadratic equation whose roots are $3\\alpha + 2$ and $2\\beta + 1$. Let's evaluate these roots:\n\n$$ \\text{First root} = 3(6) + 2 = 18 + 2 = 20 $$\n\n$$ \\text{Second root} = 2(10) + 1 = 20 + 1 = 21 $$\n\nNow, let's find the sum and the product of the roots for our new quadratic equation:\n\n$$ \\text{Sum of roots} = 20 + 21 = 41 $$\n\n$$ \\text{Product of roots} = 20 \\times 21 = 420 $$\n\nThe standard form of a quadratic equation is $x^2 - (\\text{Sum of roots})x + (\\text{Product of roots}) = 0$.\n\nSubstituting the values:\n\n$$ x^2 - 41x + 420 = 0 $$\n\nHence, the correct option is Option B.",
