      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 8th April Evening Shift. Correct option: D. Here $f(x)=\\mathrm{e}^{\\sin |x|}-|x|$. Since $|x|$ is not differentiable at $x=0$, and also $\\sin|x|$ has a “kink” at $x=0$, the function $f$ is not differentiable at $x=0$. So, Statement I is false .\n\nNow take $x \\in\\left(-\\pi,-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$. Here $x $$ \\begin{aligned} & f(x)=e^{-\\sin x}+x \\\\\\\\ & f^{\\prime}(x)=-e^{-\\sin x} \\cos x+1 \\\\\\\\ & \\text { for } x \\in\\left(-\\pi, \\frac{-\\pi}{2}\\right) \\quad \\cos x 0 \\\\\\\\ & \\therefore f(x) \\text { is increasing } \\end{aligned} $$\n\nSince $\\cos x 0$, we get $-e^{-\\sin x}\\cos x>0$, so $f'(x)>0$ on this interval. Hence, Statement II is true .",
