      "a": 2,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Morning Shift. Correct option: C. First check continuity at $x=0$:\n\n$\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0}\\left|\\frac{\\sin x}{x}\\right|=1=f(0) \\rightarrow f(x)$ is continuous\n\nFor $x \\neq 0$, write $g(x)=\\frac{\\sin x}{x}$. Then\n\nNow $\\frac{d}{d x}\\left(\\frac{\\sin x}{x}\\right)=\\frac{x \\cos x-\\sin x}{x^2}$\n\nTo find critical points, we look for points in $(-2\\pi,2\\pi)$ where either $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist.\n\nAt points where $\\frac{\\sin x}{x} \\neq 0$, the absolute value does not create a corner, so we can use $g'(x)=0$ to get stationary points:\n\n$\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{f}^{\\prime}(\\mathrm{x})=0 \\Rightarrow \\tan \\mathrm{x}=\\mathrm{x}$\n\nThe equation $\\tan x=x$ has 3 solutions in $(-2\\pi,2\\pi)$ (one is $x=0$, and one each in $(\\pi, \\tfrac{3\\pi}{2})$ and $(-\\tfrac{3\\pi}{2},-\\pi)$):\n\n[Diagram: JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Morning Shift Mathematics - Application of Derivatives Question 4 English Explanation]\n\nNext, $f'(x)$ does not exist at the zeros of $\\left|\\frac{\\sin x}{x}\\right|$, because $|\\,\\cdot\\,|$ creates a sharp corner when the inside changes sign. Here $\\sin x=0$ gives $x=\\pm \\pi$ (and $\\pm 2\\pi$ etc.). In the open interval $(-2\\pi,2\\pi)$, only $x=-\\pi$ and $x=\\pi$ lie inside.\n\nSo we have $3$ critical points from $\\tan x=x$, and $2$ more critical points where $f'(x)$ does not exist at $x=-\\pi,\\pi$.\n\ntotal 5 points",
      "source": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Morning Shift | Application of Derivatives | mnrj9y6s",
