      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Morning Shift. Correct option: C. Let the degree of the real polynomial $f$ be $n$.\n\nSince $f(x)=f^{\\prime}(x)f^{\\prime\\prime}(x)$ for all $x$, we compare degrees:\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} & \\deg(f)=\\deg(f^{\\prime})+\\deg(f^{\\prime\\prime}) \\\\ & n=(n-1)+(n-2)=2n-3 \\\\ & \\Rightarrow n=3 \\end{aligned} $$\n\nSo $f$ must be a cubic polynomial. Write\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} & f(x)=a x^3+b x^2+c x+d \\end{aligned} $$\n\nGiven $f(0)=0$, we get $d=0$. Hence\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} & f(x)=a x^3+b x^2+c x \\end{aligned} $$\n\nNow find derivatives:\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} & f^{\\prime}(x)=3 a x^2+2 b x+c \\\\ & f^{\\prime \\prime}(x)=6 a x+2 b \\end{aligned} $$\n\nUse the given condition $f(x)=f^{\\prime}(x)f^{\\prime\\prime}(x)$:\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} & \\left(a x^3+b x^2+c x\\right)=\\left(3 a x^2+2 b x+c\\right)(6 a x+2 b) \\end{aligned} $$\n\nNow compare coefficients of like powers of $x$.\n\nFor $x^3$:\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} & a=18 a^2 \\\\ & \\Rightarrow a=\\frac{1}{18}\\ \\ (\\text{since }a\\neq 0\\text{ for a cubic}) \\end{aligned} $$\n\nFor $x^2$:\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} & b=6 a b+12 a b \\\\ & b=18ab \\end{aligned} $$\n\nWith $a=\\frac{1}{18}$, this becomes $b=b$, so it gives no new condition on $b$.\n\nFor $x$:\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} & c=4 b^2+6 a c \\\\ & c=4b^2+\\frac{c}{3} \\quad (\\because 6a=\\frac{1}{3})\\\\ & \\Rightarrow \\frac{2c}{3}=4b^2 \\Rightarrow c=6b^2 \\end{aligned} $$\n\nFor the constant term:\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} & 0=2bc \\Rightarrow bc=0 \\end{aligned} $$\n\nBut $c=6b^2$, so $bc=b(6b^2)=6b^3=0 \\Rightarrow b=0$, and then $c=0$.\n\nSo the polynomial is\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} & f(x)=\\frac{1}{18}x^3 \\end{aligned} $$\n\nNow compute the required value:\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} & f^{\\prime}(x)=\\frac{1}{6}x^2 \\Rightarrow f^{\\prime}(2)=\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 4=\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ & f^{\\prime\\prime}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}x \\Rightarrow f^{\\prime\\prime}(2)=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2=\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ & \\int_0^2 f(x)\\,dx=\\int_0^2 \\frac{1}{18}x^3\\,dx=\\frac{1}{18}\\left[\\frac{x^4}{4}\\right]_0^2=\\frac{1}{18}\\cdot \\frac{16}{4}=\\frac{2}{9} \\end{aligned} $$\n\nAdd them:\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} & f^{\\prime}(2)+f^{\\prime\\prime}(2)+\\int_0^2 f(x)\\,dx \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{9}=\\frac{14}{9} \\end{aligned} $$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} & 36\\left(f^{\\prime}(2)+f^{\\prime\\prime}(2)+\\int_0^2 f(x)\\,dx\\right)=36\\cdot \\frac{14}{9}=56 \\end{aligned} $$",
