      "a": 1,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 6th April Evening Shift. Correct option: B. Since $A$ is a singular matrix, its determinant must be zero.\n\nWe have\n\n$$ A=\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 3 & -1\\\\ 2 & 1 & \\alpha\\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\end{bmatrix} $$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ |A|=0 $$\n\nNow let us calculate the determinant by expanding along the first row:\n\n$$ |A|=1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\alpha\\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} -3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & \\alpha\\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} +(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1\\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} $$\n\n$$ =1[(1)(-1)-(\\alpha)(1)]-3[(2)(-1)-(\\alpha)(0)]-1[(2)(1)-(1)(0)] $$\n\n$$ =(-1-\\alpha)-3(-2)-2 $$\n\n$$ =-1-\\alpha+6-2 $$\n\n$$ =3-\\alpha $$\n\nSince $|A|=0$,\n\n$$ 3-\\alpha=0 \\Rightarrow \\alpha=3 $$\n\nNow,\n\n$$ f(x)=\\int_0^x (t^2+2t+3)\\,dt $$\n\nIntegrating,\n\n$$ f(x)=\\left[\\frac{t^3}{3}+t^2+3t\\right]_0^x =\\frac{x^3}{3}+x^2+3x $$\n\nWe need maximum and minimum values of $f(x)$ in $[1,\\alpha]=[1,3]$.\n\nNow check whether $f(x)$ is increasing or decreasing.\n\n$$ f'(x)=x^2+2x+3 $$\n\n$$ f'(x)=x^2+2x+3=(x+1)^2+2>0 \\quad \\text{for all } x $$\n\nSo $f(x)$ is strictly increasing on $[1,3]$.\n\nHence,\n\nminimum value at $x=1$\n\nmaximum value at $x=3$\n\nNow,\n\n$$ m=f(1)=\\frac{1}{3}+1+3=\\frac{13}{3} $$\n\nand\n\n$$ M=f(3)=\\frac{27}{3}+9+9=9+9+9=27 $$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$ M-m=27-\\frac{13}{3} =\\frac{81-13}{3} =\\frac{68}{3} $$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ 3(M-m)=3\\cdot \\frac{68}{3}=68 $$\n\nHence the correct answer is:\n\n$$ \\boxed{68} $$\n\nSo, the correct option is $\\boxed{\\text{Option B}}$.",
