      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 8th April Evening Shift. Correct option: D. The function $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{x})$ is continuous at $\\boldsymbol{x}=\\frac{\\boldsymbol{\\pi}}{2}$ where\n\n$\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{x})=\\left\\{\\begin{array}{ll}\\frac{1}{3} & ; x \\leqslant \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\\\ \\frac{b(1-\\sin x)}{(\\boldsymbol{\\pi}-2 x)^2} & ; x>\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\end{array} \\cdots(i)\\right.$.\n\nWe observe the LHL from equation $(i)$ :\n\n$$ \\lim\\limits_{x \\rightarrow\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^{-}} f(x)=f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{3} \\ldots \\ldots(i i) $$\n\nWe must have the RHL from equation (i) to be $\\frac{1}{3}$ for the function $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{x})$ to be continuous at $\\boldsymbol{x}=\\frac{\\boldsymbol{\\pi}}{2}$ :\n\n$$ \\lim\\limits_{\\boldsymbol{x} \\rightarrow\\left(\\frac{\\boldsymbol{\\pi}}{2}\\right)^{+}} \\boldsymbol{f ( x )}=\\frac{1}{3} \\Rightarrow \\lim\\limits _{\\boldsymbol{x} \\rightarrow\\left(\\frac{\\boldsymbol{\\pi}}{2}\\right)^{+}} \\frac{\\boldsymbol{b}(1-\\sin \\boldsymbol{x})}{(\\boldsymbol{\\pi}-2 x)^2}=\\frac{1}{3} \\ldots \\ldots(\\boldsymbol{i i i}) $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $\\lim\\limits_{x \\rightarrow\\left(\\frac{\\boldsymbol{\\pi}}{2}\\right)^{+}} \\frac{\\boldsymbol{b}(1-\\sin \\boldsymbol{x})}{(\\boldsymbol{\\pi}-2 \\boldsymbol{x})^2}=\\frac{0}{0}$ format $\\leftarrow$ Apply L'hôpital's rule\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $\\lim\\limits_{x \\rightarrow\\left(\\frac{\\boldsymbol{\\pi}}{2}\\right)^{+}} \\frac{b \\cos \\boldsymbol{x}}{4(\\boldsymbol{\\pi}-2 \\boldsymbol{x})}=\\frac{0}{0}$ format ค Apply L'hôpital's rule once more\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $\\lim\\limits_{x \\rightarrow\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^{+}} \\frac{b \\sin x}{8}=\\frac{b}{8} \\ldots \\ldots(i v)$\n\nWe can mathematically demonstrate conditional continuity of $f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ from equation (iii) and (iv) :\n\n$$ \\frac{b}{8}=\\frac{1}{3} \\Rightarrow b=\\frac{8}{3} \\ldots \\ldots(v) $$\n\nNow, I = $\\int\\limits_0^{3 \\mathrm{~b}-6}\\left|x^2+2 x-3\\right| \\mathrm{d} x$\n\n$=\\int\\limits_0^{(3)\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)-6}\\left|\\boldsymbol{x}^2+2 \\boldsymbol{x}-3\\right| \\boldsymbol{d} \\boldsymbol{x}$\n\n$=\\int\\limits_0^2\\left|x^2+2 x-3\\right| d x$\n\n$=\\int\\limits_0^2|(x+3)(x-1)| d x$\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{array}{l}(x+3)(x-1) \\geqslant 0 ; x \\in(-\\infty,-3] \\\\ (x+3)(x-1) \\leqslant 0 ; x \\in[-3,1] \\\\ (x+3)(x-1) \\geqslant 0 ; x \\in[1, \\infty)\\end{array}\\right.$\n\n$=\\int\\limits_0^1(x+3)(1-x) d x$ $+\\int\\limits_1^2(x+3)(x-1) d x$\n\n$=\\int\\limits_0^1\\left(3-2 x-x^2\\right) d x$ $+\\int\\limits_1^2\\left(x^2+2 x-3\\right) d x$\n\n$\\begin{aligned} & =\\left[3 x-x^2-\\frac{x^3}{3}\\right]_0^1 +\\left[\\frac{x^3}{3}+x^2-3 x\\right]_1^2\\end{aligned}$\n\n$\\begin{aligned} & =\\left(3-1-\\frac{1}{3}\\right) +\\left(\\frac{8}{3}+4-6\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}+1-3\\right)\\end{aligned}$\n\n= 4",
