      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Morning Shift. Correct option: D. $$\\int \\frac{dy}{y+1}=-\\int \\frac{\\cos x}{1+\\sin x}\\,dx$$\n\nOn the left side, $\\int \\frac{dy}{y+1}=\\ln (y+1)$.\n\nOn the right side, take $1+\\sin x$ as substitution: let $u=1+\\sin x$, then $du=\\cos x\\,dx$. So, $$-\\int \\frac{\\cos x}{1+\\sin x}\\,dx=-\\int \\frac{du}{u}=-\\ln(1+\\sin x).$$\n\nSo we get $$\\Rightarrow \\ln (y+1)=-\\ln (1+\\sin x)+C$$\n\nNow use the initial condition $y(0)=0$. Put $x=0$ and $y=0$ in the equation:\n\n$$\\therefore y(0)=0 \\rightarrow \\ln(0+1)=-\\ln(1+\\sin 0)+C$$\n\nThat is $$0=-\\ln(1)+C \\Rightarrow C=0.$$\n\nSo $$\\Rightarrow \\ln (y+1)=-\\ln (1+\\sin x)$$\n\nTaking antilog on both sides, $$\\Rightarrow y+1=\\frac{1}{1+\\sin x}$$\n\nGiven that the curve passes through $$\\left(\\alpha,-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$$, substitute $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\alpha$:\n\n$$-\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{1}{1+\\sin \\alpha}$$\n\nSo $$\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{1+\\sin \\alpha} \\Rightarrow 1+\\sin \\alpha=2 \\Rightarrow \\sin \\alpha=1$$\n\nNow $\\sin \\alpha=1$ gives $$\\Rightarrow \\alpha=\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\text{ (as per options)}$$",
