      "a": 3,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 6th April Morning Shift. Correct option: D. The Work-Energy Theorem states that the total work done by all forces (conservative and non-conservative) on a particle is equal to the change in its kinetic energy.\n\n$$ \\mathrm{W}_{\\text {total }}=\\Delta \\mathrm{K} $$\n\nWhen rain drop is falling, it experiences gravitational force (vertically downward) and resistive force which opposes motion of rain drop (vertically upward).\n\n[Diagram: JEE Main 2026 (Online) 6th April Morning Shift Physics - Work Power & Energy Question 3 English Explanation]\n\n$$ \\mathrm{W}_{\\text {gravity }}+\\mathrm{W}_{\\text {resistive }}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\text {final }}-\\mathrm{K}_{\\text {initial }} $$\n\nWork done by gravity when an object falls a height h is:\n\n$$ \\mathrm{W}_{\\mathrm{g}}=\\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{g}} \\mathrm{~h}=\\mathrm{mgh} $$\n\nThe mass of the rain drop is $\\mathrm{m}=1 \\mathrm{~g}=1 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~kg}$\n\nThe height of drop is $\\mathrm{h}=1 \\mathrm{~km}=1000 \\mathrm{~m}$\n\nAnd the acceleration due to gravity is $\\mathrm{g}=10 \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s}^2$\n\nPutting the values,\n\n$$ W_g=\\left(10^{-3}\\right) \\times(10) \\times(1000)=10 \\mathrm{~J} $$\n\nThe initial velocity is $u=0$ as it started from rest, so the initial kinetic energy is $K_{\\text {initial }}=\\frac{1}{2} m u^2=0$.\n\nThe final velocity when rain drop hits the ground is $\\mathrm{v}=5 \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s}$.\n\nThus, the final kinetic energy of the rain drop is\n\n$$ \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{final}}=\\frac{1}{2} \\mathrm{mv}^2 $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $K_{f i n a l}=\\frac{1}{2} \\times\\left(10^{-3}\\right) \\times(5)^2$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\mathrm{K}_{\\text {final }}=\\frac{1}{2} \\times 10^{-3} \\times 25=12.5 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~J}=0.0125 \\mathrm{~J} $$\n\nUsing the work-energy theorem equation,\n\n$$ \\mathrm{W}_{\\mathrm{g}}+\\mathrm{W}_{\\mathrm{r}}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{final}}-\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{initial}} $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $10 \\mathrm{~J}+\\mathrm{W}_{\\mathrm{r}}=0.0125 \\mathrm{~J}$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $\\mathrm{W}_{\\mathrm{r}}=0.0125-10$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\mathrm{W}_{\\mathrm{r}}=-9.9875 \\mathrm{~J} $$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the work done by the resistive force is approximately 9.98 J .\n\nThus, the correct option is (D).",
      "source": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 6th April Morning Shift | Work, Power and Energy | mnswht3z",
