      "a": 3,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Evening Shift. Correct option: D. Young's Modulus (Y) is defined as the ratio of tensile stress to tensile strain :\n\n$$ \\mathrm{Y}=\\frac{\\text { Stress }}{\\text { Strain }}=\\frac{\\mathrm{F} / \\mathrm{A}}{\\Delta \\mathrm{~L} / \\mathrm{L}} $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\Delta \\mathrm{L}=\\frac{\\mathrm{FL}}{\\mathrm{AY}} \\ldots (ii)$$\n\nWhere, F is the tension in the string, L is the original length, A is the area of cross-section. Y is the Young's Modulus.\n\n[Diagram: JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Evening Shift Physics - Properties of Matter Question 3 English Explanation]\n\nFor string B\n\nThis string supports only the bottom block of mass 2 M .\n\n$$ \\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{B}}=2 \\mathrm{Mg} $$\n\nFor string A\n\nThis string supports both mass M and the mass 2 M hanging below it.\n\n$$ \\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{A}}=\\mathrm{Mg}+2 \\mathrm{Mg}=3 \\mathrm{Mg} $$\n\nThe ratio of lengths is $\\frac{\\mathrm{L}_{\\mathrm{A}}}{\\mathrm{L}_{\\mathrm{B}}}=2$\n\nThe ratio of Young's moduli is $\\frac{\\mathrm{Y}_{\\mathrm{A}}}{\\mathrm{Y}_{\\mathrm{B}}}=0.5$.\n\nThe areas are same, $A_A=A_B=A$.\n\nUsing the formula of extension in string (i),\n\n$$ \\frac{\\Delta L_A}{\\Delta L_B}=\\frac{F_A L_A / A_A Y_A}{F_B L_B / A_B Y_B}=\\left(\\frac{F_A}{F_B}\\right) \\times\\left(\\frac{L_A}{L_B}\\right)\\left(\\frac{Y_B}{Y_A}\\right) $$\n\nSubstituting the values :\n\n$$ \\frac{\\Delta \\mathrm{L}_{\\mathrm{A}}}{\\Delta \\mathrm{~L}_{\\mathrm{B}}}=\\left(\\frac{3 \\mathrm{Mg}}{2 \\mathrm{Mg}}\\right) \\times(2) \\times\\left(\\frac{1}{0.5}\\right) $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $\\frac{\\Delta \\mathrm{L}_{\\mathrm{A}}}{\\Delta \\mathrm{L}_{\\mathrm{B}}}=\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) \\times 2 \\times 2$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\frac{\\Delta \\mathrm{L}_{\\mathrm{A}}}{\\Delta \\mathrm{~L}_{\\mathrm{B}}}=3 \\times 2=6 $$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of elongations in A to B is 6.\n\nThus, the correct option is (D).",
      "source": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Evening Shift | Mechanical Properties of Solids | mnswhxdl",
