      "a": 0,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Evening Shift. Correct option: A. The mass of a spherical droplet is $\\mathrm{m}=\\mathrm{V} \\times \\rho$, where V is the volume and $\\rho$ is the density.\n\nThe radius of droplet is $\\mathrm{r}=1 \\mathrm{~mm}=10^{-3} \\mathrm{~m}=0.1 \\mathrm{~cm}$ and the density is $\\rho=1.5 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{cm}^3=1500 \\mathrm{~kg} / \\mathrm{m}^3$\n\nSo, the mass of spherical droplet is $\\mathrm{m}=\\frac{4}{3} \\pi \\mathrm{r}^3 \\rho=\\frac{4}{3} \\pi\\left(10^{-3}\\right)^3(1500)$\n\n$$ \\mathrm{m}=\\frac{4}{3} \\pi\\left(10^{-9}\\right)(1500)=2 \\pi \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{~kg} $$\n\n[Diagram: JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Evening Shift Physics - Electrostatics Question 8 English Explanation]\n\nSo, the gravitational force acting on it is vertically downward,\n\n$$ \\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{g}}=\\mathrm{mg}=\\left(2 \\pi \\times 10^{-6}\\right) \\times 10=2 \\pi \\times 10^{-5} \\mathrm{~N} $$\n\nThe upward force is due to electric force $\\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{e}}=\\mathrm{qE}$.\n\nFor the droplet to be at rest, the net force acting on it should be zero.\n\n$$ \\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{e}}=\\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{g}} $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\mathrm{qE}=\\mathrm{mg} \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{E}=\\frac{\\mathrm{mg}}{\\mathrm{q}} $$\n\nGiven charge on the droplet as $\\mathrm{q}=5 \\mathrm{nC}=5 \\times 10^{-9} \\mathrm{C}$ :\n\n$$ \\mathrm{E}=\\frac{2 \\pi \\times 10^{-5}}{5 \\times 10^{-9}}=\\frac{2 \\pi}{5} \\times 10^4=0.4 \\pi \\times 10^4=4000 \\pi \\mathrm{~V} / \\mathrm{m} $$\n\nThe electric field between plates with separation $\\mathrm{d}=12 \\pi \\mathrm{~cm}=0.12 \\pi \\mathrm{~m}$ is :\n\n$$ \\Delta V=E \\times d=(4000 \\pi) \\times(0.12 \\pi) $$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $$ \\Delta \\mathrm{V}=4000 \\times 0.12=480 \\mathrm{~V} $$\n\nSince the charge is positive ( 5 nC ) and the force must be upward, the electric field must point upward. This means the bottom plate (B) must be at a higher potential than the top plate (A).\n\n$$ V_B-V_A=480 \\mathrm{~V} $$\n\nSo, if $\\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{B}}=580 \\mathrm{~V}$ and $\\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{A}}=100 \\mathrm{~V}$ then the potential difference between the plates will be 480 V and the direction of electric field will be upward.\n\nFor, $\\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{B}}=100$ and $\\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{A}}=580 \\mathrm{~V}$ the potential difference between the plates is still 480 V but the direction of electric field will be downward, which doesn't satisfy the condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct option (A).",
      "source": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Evening Shift | Electrostatics | mnrr3qcm",
      "pyq": true,
      "exactPaperText": true,
      "pyqSourceSlug": "electrostatics"
