      "a": 0,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 5th April Evening Shift. Correct option: A. In a DC circuit, at steady state a capacitor is fully charged and it acts as an open circuit i.e., no current flows through the branch containing the capacitor.\n\nThe circuit simplifies to a single loop consisting of the 2 V battery, the $6 \\Omega$ resistor, and the $2 \\Omega$ resistor.\n\n[Diagram: JEE Main 2026 (Online) 5th April Evening Shift Physics - Capacitor Question 2 English Explanation]\n\nAs both the resistors are in series,\n\n$$ \\mathrm{R}_{\\text {total }}=6 \\Omega+2 \\Omega=8 \\Omega $$\n\nThe steady-state current i flowing through this main loop is:\n\n$$ \\mathrm{I}=\\frac{\\mathrm{V}}{\\mathrm{R}_{\\text {total }}}=\\frac{2}{8}=0.25 \\mathrm{~A} $$\n\nThe capacitor branch is connected in parallel with the $2 \\Omega$ resistor. Therefore, the potential difference across it is equal to the potential difference across the $2 \\Omega$ resistor.\n\n$$ \\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{BE}}=\\mathrm{i} \\times 2 \\Omega=0.25 \\times 2 \\Omega=0.5 \\mathrm{~V} $$\n\nThe total potential difference $\\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{BE}}$ is equal to that between the capacitor and the $4 \\Omega$ resistor. Since there is no current flowing through BE in steady state, the potential drop across the $4 \\Omega$ resistor is:\n\n$$ \\mathrm{V}_4=\\mathrm{i}_{\\mathrm{BE}} \\times 4 \\Omega=0 \\times 4=0 \\mathrm{~V} $$\n\nTherefore, the entire potential difference of BE is equal to that across the capacitor:\n\n$$ V_C=V_{B E}=0.5 \\mathrm{~V} $$\n\nTherefore, the potential difference across the capacitor is 0.5 V .\n\nThus, the correct option is (A).",
