      "a": 1,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 5th April Morning Shift. Correct option: B. In Young's Double Slit Experiment (YDSE), the fringe width ( $\\beta$ ) is given by :\n\n$$ \\beta=\\frac{\\lambda D}{d} $$\n\nWhere, $\\lambda$ is the wavelength of light used, D is the distance between the slits and the screen, and d is the distance between the two slits.\n\nWhen the experiment is moved from air/vacuum to a medium with a refractive index $\\mu$, the speed of light decreases, which in turn decreases the wavelength. The new wavelength $\\lambda^{\\prime}$ is:\n\n$$ \\lambda^{\\prime}=\\frac{\\lambda}{\\mu} $$\n\nSince D and d are constant for the experimental setup, the new fringe width $\\beta^{\\prime}$ in the medium is :\n\n$$ \\beta^{\\prime}=\\frac{\\lambda^{\\prime} D}{d}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\lambda}{\\mu}\\right) D}{d}=\\frac{1}{\\mu}\\left(\\frac{\\lambda D}{d}\\right) $$\n\nTherefore $\\beta^{\\prime}=\\frac{\\beta}{\\mu}$\n\nThe original fringe width (when setup is in air) is $\\beta=2.4 \\mu \\mathrm{~m}$.\n\nThe refractive index of the medium is $\\mu=1.2$.\n\nPutting the values,\n\n$$ \\beta^{\\prime}=\\frac{2.4}{1.2}=2.0 \\mu \\mathrm{~m} $$\n\nTherefore, the correct option is (B).",
      "source": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 5th April Morning Shift | Wave Optics | mnswhyj9",
