      "a": 2,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 5th April Evening Shift. Correct option: C. The de Broglie wavelength ( $\\lambda$ ) of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by:\n\n$$ \\lambda=\\frac{\\mathrm{h}}{\\mathrm{mv}} $$\n\nWhere h is Planck's constant.\n\nFor a particle of constant mass (like an electron), the wavelength is inversely proportional to its velocity:\n\n$$ \\lambda \\propto \\frac{1}{v} $$\n\nLet the initial velocity in free space be $\\mathrm{v}_0$.\n\nThe velocity in the medium ( $\\mathrm{v}_{\\mathrm{m}}$ ) is reduced by $20 \\%$ :\n\n$$ \\mathrm{v}_{\\mathrm{m}}=\\mathrm{v}_0-20 \\% \\text { of } \\mathrm{v}_0=\\mathrm{v}_0-0.20 \\mathrm{v}_0=0.80 \\mathrm{v}_0 $$\n\nLet $\\lambda_0$ be the wavelength in free space and $\\lambda_{\\mathrm{m}}$ be the wavelength in the medium.\n\n$$ \\frac{\\lambda_{\\mathrm{m}}}{\\lambda_0}=\\frac{\\mathrm{v}_0}{\\mathrm{v}_{\\mathrm{m}}} $$\n\nSubstituting the value of $\\mathrm{v}_{\\mathrm{m}}$ :\n\n$\\frac{\\lambda_{\\mathrm{m}}}{\\lambda_0}=\\frac{\\mathrm{v}_0}{0.80 \\mathrm{v}_0}=\\frac{1}{0.80}$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $\\frac{\\lambda_{\\mathrm{m}}}{\\lambda_0}=\\frac{100}{80}=1.25$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$ $\\lambda=1.25 \\lambda_0=\\alpha \\lambda_0$\n\n$$\\Rightarrow $$$$ \\alpha=1.25 $$\n\nTherefore, the correct option is (C).",
      "source": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 5th April Evening Shift | Dual Nature of Matter | mnswhvb5",
      "pyq": true,
      "exactPaperText": true,
      "pyqSourceSlug": "dual-nature-of-radiation"
