      "a": 1,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Morning Shift. Correct option: B. The input voltage is a sine wave with a peak value of 20 V .\n\nThe output voltage is clipped at +5 V during the positive half-cycle and at -5 V during the negative half-cycle.\n\nThe clipping levels are symmetrical and equal to the Zener voltage ( $\\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{Z}}=5 \\mathrm{~V}$ )\n\nTo clip both halves of the waveform at the Zener voltage, two Zener diodes must be connected in seriesopposing (back-to-back) order.\n\nFor positive half-cycle ( $\\mathrm{v}_{\\text {in }}>5 \\mathrm{~V}$ ):\n\nOne Zener diode becomes forward-biased (acting like a regular diode with a drop of $\\approx 0 \\mathrm{~V}$ for an ideal case, while the other reaches its breakdown region $\\left(\\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{Z}}=5 \\mathrm{~V}\\right)$. The total voltage drop across the pair is 5 V , which appears as the output $\\mathrm{v}_{\\mathrm{o}}$.\n\nFor negative half-cycle ( $\\mathrm{v}_{\\mathrm{in}} The roles are reversed. The diode that was in breakdown now becomes forward-biased, and the other reaches its breakdown region at -5 V .\n\nIn part (A) of the given, the circuit already contains an ideal diode and a Zener diode in series. However, a standard symmetrical clipper that produces the output shown in part (C) typically uses two Zener diodes connected back-to-back.\n\nLooking at the options, the circuit that corresponds to this clipping at $\\pm 5 \\mathrm{~V}$ is a pair of Zener diodes connected in series with their anodes or cathodes facing each other.\n\nSo, based on the output waveform , the correct component configuration between X and Y is the one that allows clipping at the Zener breakdown level in both directions.\n\nThus, the component between X and Y be with two diodes in opposite directions. Hence, the correct option is (B).",
      "source": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Morning Shift | Semiconductors | mnrkosi9",
      "pyq": true,
