      "a": 1,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 5th April Evening Shift. Correct option: B. Statement A: If two orbitals have the same value of $(n+l)$, the orbital with lower value of $n$ will have lower energy.\n\nAccording to the Bohr-Bury rule (or the $(n+l)$ rule) for the filling of electrons in multi-electron atoms:\n\nOrbitals with a lower value of $(n+l)$ have lower energy.\n\nIf two orbitals have the same $(n+l)$ value, the orbital with the lower principal quantum number ($n$) will have the lower energy.\n\nFor example, for the $3\\mathrm{d}$ orbital, $(n+l) = 3+2 = 5$. For the $4\\mathrm{p}$ orbital, $(n+l) = 4+1 = 5$. Since $3\\mathrm{d}$ has a lower value of $n$ ($n=3$) compared to $4\\mathrm{p}$ ($n=4$), the $3\\mathrm{d}$ orbital has lower energy.\n\nTherefore, Statement A is true .\n\nStatement B: Energies of the orbitals in the same subshell increase with increase in atomic number.\n\nAs the atomic number ($Z$) increases, the nuclear charge increases. This leads to a stronger electrostatic pull on the electrons by the nucleus, which increases the effective nuclear charge ($Z_{\\mathrm{eff}}$). Due to this stronger attraction, the electrons are held more tightly, and the energy of the orbitals becomes more negative (it decreases, not increases). For example, the energy of a $2\\mathrm{s}$ orbital follows the order: $E_{2\\mathrm{s}}(\\mathrm{H}) > E_{2\\mathrm{s}}(\\mathrm{Li}) > E_{2\\mathrm{s}}(\\mathrm{Na})$.\n\nTherefore, Statement B is false .\n\nStatement C: The size of $2\\mathrm{p}_x$ orbital is less than the size of $3\\mathrm{p}_x$ orbital.\n\nThe size of an orbital is primarily determined by its principal quantum number ($n$). As the value of $n$ increases, the distance of the electron from the nucleus increases, making the orbital larger. Since $n=3$ for the $3\\mathrm{p}_x$ orbital and $n=2$ for the $2\\mathrm{p}_x$ orbital, the $3\\mathrm{p}_x$ orbital is larger than the $2\\mathrm{p}_x$ orbital.\n\nTherefore, Statement C is true .\n\nStatement D: Among $5\\mathrm{f}, 6\\mathrm{s}, 4\\mathrm{d}, 5\\mathrm{p}$ and $5\\mathrm{d}$ orbitals, none of the orbitals have 2 radial nodes.\n\nThe number of radial nodes for any orbital is calculated using the formula: $(n - l - 1)$.\n\nLet us calculate the radial nodes for the given orbitals:\n\nFor $5\\mathrm{f}$ ($n=5, l=3$): Radial nodes $= 5 - 3 - 1 = 1$\n\nFor $6\\mathrm{s}$ ($n=6, l=0$): Radial nodes $= 6 - 0 - 1 = 5$\n\nFor $4\\mathrm{d}$ ($n=4, l=2$): Radial nodes $= 4 - 2 - 1 = 1$\n\nFor $5\\mathrm{p}$ ($n=5, l=1$): Radial nodes $= 5 - 1 - 1 = 3$\n\nFor $5\\mathrm{d}$ ($n=5, l=2$): Radial nodes $= 5 - 2 - 1 = 2$\n\nAs we can see, the $5\\mathrm{d}$ orbital indeed has exactly 2 radial nodes. Thus, the statement that none of them has 2 radial nodes is incorrect.\n\nTherefore, Statement D is false .\n\nConclusion:\n\nOnly statements A and C are true.\n\nThe correct answer is Option B .",
