      "a": 0,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 5th April Evening Shift. Correct option: A. 1. Finding the Covalent Bond Length:\n\nIn chemistry, the covalent bond length is defined as the average distance between the centers of the nuclei of two bonded atoms in a molecule.\n\nWhen atoms $A$ and $B$ form a single covalent bond, we can approximate the distance between their nuclei by simply adding their individual covalent radii.\n\nTherefore, if the covalent radius of atom $A$ is $r_A$ and the covalent radius of atom $B$ is $r_B$, the internuclear distance, which is the bond length of the $AB$ molecule, is:\n\n$$ \\text{Bond Length} = r_A + r_B $$\n\n2. Finding the Total Length of the AB Molecule:\n\nTo find the total length of the entire molecule, we need to consider the full physical extent of both atoms. Imagine the two atoms as spheres placed side by side on a straight axis.\n\nThe center of atom $A$ to its outermost left edge is equal to its radius, $r_A$.\n\nThe distance from the center of atom $A$ to the center of atom $B$ is the bond length, which we already established is $(r_A + r_B)$.\n\nThe center of atom $B$ to its outermost right edge is equal to its radius, $r_B$.\n\nThe total length of the molecule is the distance from the outermost left edge of atom $A$ to the outermost right edge of atom $B$. We can find this by adding the three segments together:\n\n$$ \\text{Total Length} = (\\text{radius of } A) + (\\text{internuclear distance}) + (\\text{radius of } B) $$\n\n$$ \\text{Total Length} = r_A + (r_A + r_B) + r_B $$\n\n$$ \\text{Total Length} = 2r_A + 2r_B $$\n\n$$ \\text{Total Length} = 2(r_A + r_B) $$\n\nSo, the covalent bond length is $(r_A + r_B)$ and the total length of the full $AB$ molecule is $2(r_A + r_B)$.\n\nComparing our findings with the given options, this corresponds perfectly to Option A.\n\nCorrect Answer:\n\nOption A\n\n$\\left(\\mathrm{r}_{\\mathrm{A}}+\\mathrm{r}_{\\mathrm{B}}\\right), 2\\left(\\mathrm{r}_{\\mathrm{A}}+\\mathrm{r}_{\\mathrm{B}}\\right)$",
