      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Morning Shift. Correct option: D. Write the given reaction data.\n\n(i) $ 2\\text{Al(s)} + 6\\text{HCl(aq)} \\rightarrow \\text{Al}_2\\text{Cl}_6\\text{(aq)} + 3\\text{H}_2\\text{(g)} $; $ \\Delta_\\text{r}H_1 = -1200~\\text{kJ/mol} $\n\n(ii) $ \\text{H}_2\\text{(g)} + \\text{Cl}_2\\text{(g)} \\rightarrow 2\\text{HCl(g)} $; $ \\Delta_\\text{r}H_2 = -164~\\text{kJ/mol} $\n\n(iii) $ \\text{HCl(g)} + \\text{aq} \\rightarrow \\text{HCl(aq)} $; $ \\Delta_\\text{r}H_3 = -83~\\text{kJ/mol} $\n\n(iv) $ \\text{Al}_2\\text{Cl}_6\\text{(s)} + \\text{aq} \\rightarrow \\text{Al}_2\\text{Cl}_6\\text{(aq)} $; $ \\Delta_\\text{r}H_4 = +663~\\text{kJ/mol} $\n\nAdjust the equations so that we can connect them to the target reaction.\n\nTarget reaction: $ 2\\text{Al(s)} + 3\\text{Cl}_2\\text{(g)} \\rightarrow \\text{Al}_2\\text{Cl}_6\\text{(s)} $\n\nThe given first reaction produces $ \\text{Al}_2\\text{Cl}_6(\\text{aq}) $ from $ 2\\text{Al} $ and $ 6\\text{HCl(aq)} $. We need to convert all species to match the target reaction, step by step.\n\nUse Hess’s Law\n\nAccording to Hess’s Law, the net enthalpy change for the target reaction is the sum of the enthalpy changes for all the necessary intermediate reactions.\n\nFirst, multiply equation (ii) by 3 to get 6 moles of HCl(g):\n\n$ 3[\\text{H}_2 + \\text{Cl}_2 \\rightarrow 2\\text{HCl(g)}] $; $ \\Delta_\\text{r}H_2 = -164 \\times 3~\\text{kJ/mol} $\n\nThen, multiply equation (iii) by 6 to convert 6 moles of HCl(g) to HCl(aq):\n\n$ 6[\\text{HCl(g)} + \\text{aq} \\rightarrow \\text{HCl(aq)}] $; $ \\Delta_\\text{r}H_3 = -83 \\times 6~\\text{kJ/mol} $\n\nWe also reverse equation (iv) to obtain $ \\text{Al}_2\\text{Cl}_6\\text{(aq)} \\rightarrow \\text{Al}_2\\text{Cl}_6\\text{(s)} $, giving $ \\Delta_\\text{r}H_4 = +663~\\text{kJ/mol} $.\n\nAdd up all enthalpy changes.\n\nUsing Hess’s Law:\n\n$$ \\Delta_\\text{r}H = \\Delta_\\text{r}H_1 + \\Delta_\\text{r}H_2 + \\Delta_\\text{r}H_3 + \\Delta_\\text{r}H_4 $$\n\nSubstitute the values:\n\n$$ \\Delta_\\text{r}H = (-1200) - (164 \\times 3) - (83 \\times 6) + 663 $$\n\nSimplify the calculation.\n\n$$ \\Delta_\\text{r}H = -1200 - 492 - 498 + 663 = -1527~\\text{kJ/mol} $$\n\nFinal Answer: The enthalpy of formation of solid anhydrous $ \\text{Al}_2\\text{Cl}_6 $ is $ \\boxed{-1527~\\text{kJ/mol}} $.",
