      "a": 1,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Evening Shift. Correct option: B. Given data:\n\nConcentration of the weak acid $HX$, $C = 0.2 \\text{ M}$\n\nInitial volume of $HX = 20.0 \\text{ mL}$\n\nConcentration of $NaOH = 0.2 \\text{ M}$\n\nDissociation constant of acid, $K_a = 5 \\times 10^{-4}$\n\n$\\mathrm{p}K_a = 3.3$\n\nCase (a): At the start of the titration (when NO $NaOH$ has been added)\n\nAt this point, only the weak acid $HX$ is present in the solution. Since the degree of dissociation $\\alpha \\ll 1$, we can calculate the hydrogen ion concentration $[H^+]$ using the formula for a weak monoprotic acid:\n\n$$ [H^+] = \\sqrt{K_a \\times C} $$\n\nSubstitute the values of $K_a$ and $C$:\n\n$$ [H^+] = \\sqrt{5 \\times 10^{-4} \\times 0.2} $$\n\n$$ [H^+] = \\sqrt{1 \\times 10^{-4}} $$\n\n$$ [H^+] = 10^{-2} \\text{ M} $$\n\nThe $\\text{pH}$ of the solution is:\n\n$$ \\text{pH} = -\\log[H^+] $$\n\n$$ \\text{pH} = -\\log(10^{-2}) = 2.0 $$\n\nCase (b): When $10 \\text{ mL}$ of $0.2 \\text{ M } NaOH$ is added\n\nWhen strong base $NaOH$ is added, it reacts with the weak acid $HX$ to form salt $NaX$ and water:\n\n$$ HX + NaOH \\longrightarrow NaX + H_2O $$\n\nLet us find the number of millimoles (mmol) of acid and base:\n\n$$ \\text{Initial millimoles of } HX = \\text{Molarity} \\times \\text{Volume in mL} = 0.2 \\text{ M} \\times 20.0 \\text{ mL} = 4.0 \\text{ mmol} $$\n\n$$ \\text{Millimoles of } NaOH \\text{ added} = 0.2 \\text{ M} \\times 10.0 \\text{ mL} = 2.0 \\text{ mmol} $$\n\nSince $NaOH$ is the limiting reagent, $2.0 \\text{ mmol}$ of $NaOH$ will completely neutralize $2.0 \\text{ mmol}$ of $HX$, forming $2.0 \\text{ mmol}$ of the salt $NaX$.\n\nLet's analyze the composition of the mixture after the reaction:\n\n$$ \\text{Remaining millimoles of } HX = 4.0 - 2.0 = 2.0 \\text{ mmol} $$\n\n$$ \\text{Millimoles of salt } NaX \\text{ formed} = 2.0 \\text{ mmol} $$\n\nNow, the solution contains a weak acid ($HX$) and its salt with a strong base ($NaX$). This constitutes an acidic buffer solution . We can determine the $\\text{pH}$ using the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation:\n\n$$ \\text{pH} = \\mathrm{p}K_a + \\log \\left( \\frac{[\\text{Salt}]}{[\\text{Acid}]} \\right) $$\n\nSince the volumes are the same, the ratio of their concentrations is simply the ratio of their millimoles:\n\n$$ \\text{pH} = 3.3 + \\log \\left( \\frac{2.0}{2.0} \\right) $$\n\n$$ \\text{pH} = 3.3 + \\log(1) $$\n\nSince $\\log(1) = 0$:\n\n$$ \\text{pH} = 3.3 + 0 = 3.3 $$\n\n(Note: We can also directly observe that because exactly half the equivalence volume of the base is added, it is the half-neutralization point, where $\\text{pH} = \\mathrm{p}K_a$.)\n\nConclusion:\n\nThe $\\text{pH}$ values for part (a) and (b) are $2.0$ and $3.3$ respectively.\n\nCorrect Option: Option B",
