      "a": 1,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Morning Shift. Correct option: B. For a zero order reaction :\n\nThe integrated rate law is\n\n$$ [A]_t = [A]_0 - kt $$\n\nFor 100% completion , $[A]_t = 0$.\n\nSo,\n\n$$ 0 = [A]_0 - kt_{100\\%} $$\n\n$$ t_{100\\%} = \\frac{[A]_0}{k} $$\n\nFor half-life , $[A]_t = \\frac{[A]_0}{2}$.\n\nSo,\n\n$$ \\frac{[A]_0}{2} = [A]_0 - kt_{1/2} $$\n\n$$ kt_{1/2} = \\frac{[A]_0}{2} $$\n\n$$ t_{1/2} = \\frac{[A]_0}{2k} $$\n\nHence,\n\n$$ t_{100\\%} = \\frac{[A]_0}{k} = 2\\left(\\frac{[A]_0}{2k}\\right) = 2t_{1/2} $$\n\nSo for zero order ,\n\n$$ t_{100\\%} = 2t_{1/2} $$\n\nFor a first order reaction :\n\nThe integrated rate law is\n\n$$ [A]_t = [A]_0 e^{-kt} $$\n\nFor 100% completion , we need $[A]_t = 0$.\n\nThat means\n\n$$ 0 = [A]_0 e^{-kt} $$\n\nBut $e^{-kt}$ can never become exactly zero for any finite value of $t$.\n\nSo, the reaction takes infinite time for complete consumption.\n\nThus,\n\n$$ t_{100\\%} = \\infty $$\n\nAlso, for first order reaction,\n\n$$ t_{1/2} = \\frac{0.693}{k} $$\n\nHence $t_{100\\%}$ is not a finite multiple of $t_{1/2}$, rather it is effectively represented as infinite.\n\nSo the correct relation is:\n\nZero order: $t_{100\\%} = 2t_{1/2}$\n\nFirst order: $t_{100\\%} = (t_{1/2})^{\\infty}$ in the given options, meaning complete reaction requires infinite time.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is:\n\n$$ \\boxed{\\text{Option B}} $$",
