      "a": 2,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 6th April Morning Shift. Correct option: C. Statement A: Basic vanadium oxide is used in the manufacture of $\\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{SO}_4$.\n\nThe manufacture of sulphuric acid ($\\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{SO}_4$) by the Contact Process is a very important reaction.\n\nIn this process, the key step is the catalytic oxidation of $\\mathrm{SO}_2$ to $\\mathrm{SO}_3$:\n\n$$ 2\\mathrm{SO}_2(g) + \\mathrm{O}_2(g) \\xrightarrow{V_2O_5} 2\\mathrm{SO}_3(g) $$\n\nThe catalyst used here is Vanadium pentoxide, $V_2O_5$.\n\nNow, we need to check the nature of this oxide. Let's find the oxidation state of Vanadium (V) in $V_2O_5$.\n\nLet the oxidation state of V be $x$.\n\n$$ 2(x) + 5(-2) = 0 $$\n\n$$ 2x - 10 = 0 $$\n\n$$ x = +5 $$\n\nAs a general rule from the d-block elements chapter, metal oxides in higher oxidation states are acidic or amphoteric in nature. Since Vanadium is in its highest oxidation state (+5), $V_2O_5$ is amphoteric, meaning it reacts with both acids and bases. It is not a basic oxide.\n\nSo, statement A is incorrect .\n\nStatement B: The spin-only magnetic moment value of the transition metal halide employed in Ziegler-Natta polymerization is 2.84 BM.\n\nZiegler-Natta catalysts are used for the polymerization of alkenes, like converting ethene to polyethene.\n\nA typical Ziegler-Natta catalyst is a mixture of titanium tetrachloride ($TiCl_4$) and triethylaluminium ($Al(C_2H_5)_3$).\n\nThe transition metal halide here is $TiCl_4$. Let's find the magnetic moment for the metal ion in this compound.\n\nIn $TiCl_4$, the oxidation state of Titanium (Ti) is +4.\n\nThe atomic number of Ti is 22. Its electronic configuration is $[Ar] 3d^2 4s^2$.\n\nThe electronic configuration of the $Ti^{4+}$ ion is $[Ar] 3d^0$.\n\nThe number of unpaired electrons ($n$) is 0.\n\nThe spin-only magnetic moment ($\\mu$) is calculated using the formula:\n\n$$ \\mu = \\sqrt{n(n+2)} \\text{ B.M.} $$\n\nFor $Ti^{4+}$, since $n=0$,\n\n$$ \\mu = \\sqrt{0(0+2)} = 0 \\text{ B.M.} $$\n\nThe value given in the statement is 2.84 B.M. A value of $\\mu \\approx 2.84$ B.M. corresponds to 2 unpaired electrons ($n=2$), because $\\sqrt{2(2+2)} = \\sqrt{8} \\approx 2.83$ B.M.\n\nSince the magnetic moment of Ti in $TiCl_4$ is 0 B.M., this statement does not hold for the common example of a Ziegler-Natta catalyst.\n\nSo, statement B is incorrect .\n\nStatement C: The p-block metal compound employed in Ziegler-Natta polymerization has the metal in +3 oxidation state.\n\nAs we just discussed, the Ziegler-Natta catalyst contains a compound like triethylaluminium, $Al(C_2H_5)_3$.\n\nAluminium (Al) belongs to Group 13, which is in the p-block of the periodic table.\n\nLet's find the oxidation state of Al in $Al(C_2H_5)_3$. The ethyl group ($C_2H_5$) is an alkyl group and carries a charge of -1 when bonded to a metal.\n\nLet the oxidation state of Al be $x$.\n\n$$ x + 3(-1) = 0 $$\n\n$$ x = +3 $$\n\nSo, the p-block metal (Al) is indeed in the +3 oxidation state.\n\nTherefore, statement C is correct .\n\nStatement D: The number of electrons present in the outer most 'd' orbital of metal halide employed in Wacker process is 8.\n\nThe Wacker process is used for the oxidation of ethene to ethanal (acetaldehyde) using a catalyst.\n\nThe catalyst used is Palladium(II) chloride, $PdCl_2$, in an aqueous medium.\n\nThe metal halide here is $PdCl_2$.\n\nIn $PdCl_2$, the oxidation state of Palladium (Pd) is +2.\n\nPalladium (Pd) has atomic number 46. Its electronic configuration is $[Kr] 4d^{10} 5s^0$.\n\nTo get the configuration of the $Pd^{2+}$ ion, we remove two electrons. The configuration becomes $[Kr] 4d^8$.\n\nThe outermost 'd' orbital for Palladium is the 4d orbital. The number of electrons in this 4d orbital is 8.\n\nTherefore, statement D is correct .\n\nConclusion:\n\nWe found that statements C and D are correct, while A and B are incorrect.\n\nLooking at the options, we need to choose the one that says \"C and D Only\".\n\nThe correct answer is Option C .",
      "source": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 6th April Morning Shift | d & f Block | mnswhrm5",
