      "a": 0,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Evening Shift. Correct option: A. For all the three complexes, the metal ion is $ \\mathrm{Ni}^{2+} $.\n\nThe electronic configuration of Ni is\n\n$$ \\mathrm{Ni} : [Ar]\\,3d^8 4s^2 $$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ \\mathrm{Ni}^{2+} : [Ar]\\,3d^8 $$\n\nNow let us examine each complex.\n\n1. Complex $(A) = [\\mathrm{Ni}(\\mathrm{en})_3]^{2+}$\n\nHere, $ \\mathrm{en} $ is ethane-1,2-diamine, a neutral bidentate ligand.\n\nCoordination number $= 6$\n\nGeometry $= $ octahedral\n\nFor an octahedral $d^8$ configuration, the arrangement is:\n\n$$ t_{2g}^6 e_g^2 $$\n\nThis gives 2 unpaired electrons .\n\nSo, for $(A)$:\n\n$$ \\text{Number of unpaired electrons} = 2 $$\n\n2. Complex $(B) = [\\mathrm{NiCl}_4]^{2-}$\n\nCoordination number $= 4$\n\nSince $ \\mathrm{Cl}^- $ is a weak ligand, this complex is tetrahedral .\n\nFor tetrahedral $d^8$, the splitting is small, and the electrons remain high spin.\n\nHence, it has 2 unpaired electrons .\n\nSo, for $(B)$:\n\n$$ \\text{Number of unpaired electrons} = 2 $$\n\n3. Complex $(C) = [\\mathrm{Ni}(\\mathrm{NH}_3)_6]^{2+}$\n\nCoordination number $= 6$\n\nGeometry $= $ octahedral\n\nAgain, for octahedral $d^8$:\n\n$$ t_{2g}^6 e_g^2 $$\n\nSo it also has 2 unpaired electrons .\n\nThus, for $(C)$:\n\n$$ \\text{Number of unpaired electrons} = 2 $$\n\nFrequency of absorption\n\nThe frequency of absorbed light depends on crystal field splitting:\n\n$$ \\Delta \\propto \\nu $$\n\nGreater the splitting, greater the frequency of absorption.\n\nNow compare ligand strengths:\n\n$$ \\mathrm{en} > \\mathrm{NH}_3 > \\mathrm{Cl}^- $$\n\nAlso, tetrahedral complexes have smaller splitting than octahedral complexes.\n\nSo,\n\n$[\\mathrm{Ni}(\\mathrm{en})_3]^{2+}$ has the highest $ \\Delta $\n\n$[\\mathrm{Ni}(\\mathrm{NH}_3)_6]^{2+}$ has next\n\n$[\\mathrm{NiCl}_4]^{2-}$ has the least\n\nTherefore, order of frequency of absorption is:\n\n$$ (A) > (C) > (B) $$\n\nHence the correct option is:\n\n$$ \\boxed{\\text{Option A}} $$",
      "source": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Evening Shift | Practical Chemistry | mnswhq2h",
      "pyq": true,
      "exactPaperText": true,
