      "a": 1,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Evening Shift. Correct option: B. Coordination Isomerism arises from the interchange of ligands between cationic and anionic entities of different metal ions present in a complex. When the complete interchange of ligands takes place, it must result in a new, chemically distinct isomer.\n\nLet us evaluate each of the given choices under this concept:\n\nA. $[\\mathrm{Ag(NH}_3)_2][\\mathrm{Ag(CN)}_2]$: Both the cationic and anionic entities contain the same metal ion ($ \\text{Ag} $). If we fully interchange the ligands, we get $[\\mathrm{Ag(CN)}_2]^- [\\mathrm{Ag(NH}_3)_2]^+$, which is exactly the same compound written in reverse order. Further, a partial interchange leads to neutral molecules $[\\mathrm{Ag(NH}_3)(\\mathrm{CN})]$, which destroys the cation-anion pair. Hence, it does not show coordination isomerism.\n\nB. $[\\mathrm{Co(NH}_3)_6][\\mathrm{Cr(CN)}_6]$: This complex contains two different metal ions ($ \\text{Co} $ and $ \\text{Cr} $). By interchanging the ligands between the cationic and anionic coordination spheres, we get a completely new distinct complex: $[\\mathrm{Cr(NH}_3)_6][\\mathrm{Co(CN)}_6]$. Hence, it shows coordination isomerism. (This is also the classic example mentioned in NCERT).\n\nC. $[\\mathrm{Co(NH}_3)_6][\\mathrm{Co(CN)}_6]$: Both entities contain the same metal ion ($ \\text{Co} $). A complete interchange of all ligands gives $[\\mathrm{Co(CN)}_6]^{3-} [\\mathrm{Co(NH}_3)_6]^{3+}$, which is exactly the same coordination compound. According to the strict NCERT definition requiring different metal ions for complete interchange, it does not exhibit coordination isomerism.\n\nD. $[\\mathrm{Fe(NH}_3)_6][\\mathrm{Co(CN)}_6]$: This complex has two different metal ions ($ \\text{Fe} $ and $ \\text{Co} $). Exchanging the primary coordination spheres yields a distinct coordination isomer: $[\\mathrm{Co(NH}_3)_6][\\mathrm{Fe(CN)}_6]$. Hence, it shows coordination isomerism.\n\nE. $[\\mathrm{Co(NH}_3)_6][\\mathrm{Fe(CN)}_6]$: This complex is exactly the coordination isomer of the complex described in option D. Since it consists of different metal ions ($ \\text{Co} $ and $ \\text{Fe} $), exchanging its ligands will yield the complex in D. Thus, it also shows coordination isomerism.\n\nConclusion:\n\nComplexes B, D, and E exhibit coordination isomerism because they possess differently named metal ions in their cationic and anionic parts, allowing the exchange of ligands to produce distinctly new isomers.\n\nCorrect Answer:\n\nOption B (B, D and E Only)",
      "source": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Evening Shift | Practical Chemistry | mnrsbi25",
      "pyq": true,
