      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Evening Shift. Correct option: D. For the equation $x^2 - 3x + r = 0$ with roots $\\alpha, \\beta$, by using the standard results:\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} & \\alpha+\\beta=3 \\\\ & \\frac{\\alpha}{2}+2 \\beta=-3 \\end{aligned} $$\n\nThe first line comes from “sum of roots” $= -\\dfrac{\\text{coefficient of }x}{\\text{coefficient of }x^2} = -\\dfrac{-3}{1}=3$.\n\nThe second line comes from the equation $x^2 + 3x + r = 0$, whose roots are $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $2\\beta$. So, their sum is:\n\n$$\\frac{\\alpha}{2} + 2\\beta = -\\frac{3}{1} = -3$$\n\nNow solve the two equations:\n\n$$\\alpha+\\beta=3 \\Rightarrow \\alpha=3-\\beta$$\n\nSubstitute into $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}+2\\beta=-3$:\n\n$$\\frac{3-\\beta}{2}+2\\beta=-3$$\n\nMultiply by $2$:\n\n$$3-\\beta+4\\beta=-6 \\Rightarrow 3+3\\beta=-6 \\Rightarrow 3\\beta=-9 \\Rightarrow \\beta=-3$$\n\nThen $\\alpha=3-(-3)=6$.\n\nNow use “product of roots” for $x^2 - 3x + r = 0$:\n\n$$\\alpha\\beta=r \\Rightarrow r=6\\times(-3)=-18$$\n\nNow consider the equation $x^2 + 6x = m$. Rewrite it in standard form:\n\n$$x^2 + 6x - m = 0$$\n\nSo, product of its roots $= \\dfrac{-m}{1}=-m$.\n\nThe roots are given as $2\\alpha + \\beta + 2r$ and $\\alpha - 2\\beta - \\dfrac{r}{2}$. Substitute $\\alpha=6$, $\\beta=-3$, $r=-18$:\n\nFirst root:\n\n$$2\\alpha + \\beta + 2r = 2(6)+(-3)+2(-18)=12-3-36=-27$$\n\nSecond root:\n\n$$\\alpha - 2\\beta - \\frac{r}{2} = 6-2(-3)-\\frac{-18}{2}=6+6+9=21$$\n\nNow product of roots:\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} & =(2 \\alpha+\\beta+2 \\mathrm{r})\\left(\\alpha-2 \\beta-\\frac{\\mathrm{r}}{2}\\right) \\\\ & \\Rightarrow-\\mathrm{m}=(-27)(21) \\\\ & \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{m}=567 \\end{aligned} $$",
