      "a": 3,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 2nd April Morning Shift. Correct option: D. We need to find the number of elements in\n\n$$ S=\\{x\\in[-\\pi,\\pi]: \\sin x(\\sin x+\\cos x)=a,\\; a\\in\\mathbb Z\\}. $$\n\nSo we must find all $x\\in[-\\pi,\\pi]$ for which the value\n\n$$ \\sin x(\\sin x+\\cos x) $$\n\nis an integer.\n\nStep 1: Simplify the expression\n\nLet\n\n$$ f(x)=\\sin x(\\sin x+\\cos x). $$\n\nThen\n\n$$ f(x)=\\sin^2 x+\\sin x\\cos x. $$\n\nNow use the identities:\n\n$$ \\sin^2 x=\\frac{1-\\cos 2x}{2}, \\qquad \\sin x\\cos x=\\frac{\\sin 2x}{2}. $$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ f(x)=\\frac{1-\\cos 2x}{2}+\\frac{\\sin 2x}{2} = \\frac{1+\\sin 2x-\\cos 2x}{2}. $$\n\nAlso,\n\n$$ \\sin 2x-\\cos 2x=\\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right). $$\n\nHence,\n\n$$ f(x)=\\frac{1+\\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)}{2}. $$\n\nStep 2: Find the range of $f(x)$\n\nSince\n\n$$ -1\\le \\sin\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\le 1, $$\n\nwe get\n\n$$ -\\sqrt{2}\\le \\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\le \\sqrt{2}. $$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$ \\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\le f(x)\\le \\frac{1+\\sqrt{2}}{2}. $$\n\nNow,\n\n$$ \\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\approx -0.207,\\qquad \\frac{1+\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\approx 1.207. $$\n\nSo the only integers possible in this interval are\n\n$$ 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 1. $$\n\nThus we need to solve:\n\n$f(x)=0$\n\n$f(x)=1$\n\nStep 3: Solve $f(x)=0$\n\nWe have\n\n$$ \\sin x(\\sin x+\\cos x)=0. $$\n\nSo either\n\nCase 1:\n\n$$ \\sin x=0 $$\n\nIn $[-\\pi,\\pi]$, this gives\n\n$$ x=-\\pi,\\;0,\\;\\pi. $$\n\nCase 2:\n\n$$ \\sin x+\\cos x=0 $$\n\nThat is,\n\n$$ \\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=0 $$\n\nso\n\n$$ x+\\frac{\\pi}{4}=n\\pi \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x=n\\pi-\\frac{\\pi}{4}. $$\n\nNow check in $[-\\pi,\\pi]$:\n\nfor $n=0$, $x=-\\frac{\\pi}{4}$\n\nfor $n=1$, $x=\\frac{3\\pi}{4}$\n\nother values go outside the interval\n\nSo from $f(x)=0$, we get\n\n$$ x=-\\pi,\\;0,\\;\\pi,\\;-\\frac{\\pi}{4},\\;\\frac{3\\pi}{4}. $$\n\nTotal $5$ values.\n\nStep 4: Solve $f(x)=1$\n\nWe need\n\n$$ \\sin x(\\sin x+\\cos x)=1. $$\n\nUsing the simplified form:\n\n$$ \\frac{1+\\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)}{2}=1. $$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ 1+\\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=2 $$\n\n$$ \\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=1 $$\n\n$$ \\sin\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}. $$\n\nNow,\n\n$$ \\sin \\theta=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} $$\n\nwhen\n\n$$ \\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{4}+2n\\pi \\quad\\text{or}\\quad \\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{4}+2n\\pi. $$\n\nSo,\n\nFirst possibility:\n\n$$ 2x-\\frac{\\pi}{4}=\\frac{\\pi}{4}+2n\\pi $$\n\n$$ 2x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+2n\\pi $$\n\n$$ x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}+n\\pi $$\n\nIn $[-\\pi,\\pi]$, this gives\n\n$$ x=\\frac{\\pi}{4},\\;-\\frac{3\\pi}{4}. $$\n\nSecond possibility:\n\n$$ 2x-\\frac{\\pi}{4}=\\frac{3\\pi}{4}+2n\\pi $$\n\n$$ 2x=\\pi+2n\\pi $$\n\n$$ x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi $$\n\nIn $[-\\pi,\\pi]$, this gives\n\n$$ x=\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\;-\\frac{\\pi}{2}. $$\n\nSo from $f(x)=1$, we get $4$ values:\n\n$$ x=\\frac{\\pi}{4},\\;-\\frac{3\\pi}{4},\\;\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\;-\\frac{\\pi}{2}. $$\n\nStep 5: Total number of elements\n\nCombining both cases:\n\nfrom $a=0$: $5$ values\n\nfrom $a=1$: $4$ values\n\nHence,\n\n$$ n(S)=5+4=9. $$\n\nSo the correct answer is:\n\n$$ \\boxed{9} $$\n\nTherefore, Option D is correct.",
