      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 6th April Evening Shift. Correct option: C. For the quadratic equation\n\n$$ \\left(n^2-2n+2\\right)x^2-3x+\\left(n^2-2n+2\\right)^2=0, $$\n\nlet us first compare it with the standard quadratic form\n\n$$ ax^2+bx+c=0. $$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ a=n^2-2n+2,\\qquad b=-3,\\qquad c=\\left(n^2-2n+2\\right)^2. $$\n\nNow rewrite $a$ in a simpler form:\n\n$$ n^2-2n+2=(n-1)^2+1. $$\n\nHence,\n\n$$ a=(n-1)^2+1. $$\n\nSince $(n-1)^2 \\ge 0$, we get\n\n$$ a \\ge 1. $$\n\nNow, for a quadratic equation, by Vieta’s formulas:\n\nSum of roots $= -\\dfrac{b}{a}$\n\nProduct of roots $= \\dfrac{c}{a}$\n\nSo here,\n\n$$ \\text{Sum of roots}=\\frac{3}{a} $$\n\nand\n\n$$ \\text{Product of roots}=\\frac{a^2}{a}=a. $$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$ \\text{Product of roots}=(n-1)^2+1. $$\n\nSo the minimum value of the product is clearly\n\n$$ \\alpha=1, $$\n\nattained when $n=1$.\n\nNow the sum of roots is\n\n$$ \\frac{3}{(n-1)^2+1}. $$\n\nSince the denominator is minimum when $(n-1)^2=0$, the maximum value of the sum is\n\n$$ \\beta=\\frac{3}{1}=3. $$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$ \\alpha=1,\\qquad \\beta=3. $$\n\nNow the G.P. has\n\nfirst term $a=\\alpha=1$\n\ncommon ratio $r=\\dfrac{\\alpha}{\\beta}=\\dfrac{1}{3}$\n\nWe need the sum of first six terms:\n\n$$ S_6=a\\cdot \\frac{1-r^6}{1-r}. $$\n\nSubstitute the values:\n\n$$ S_6=1\\cdot \\frac{1-\\left(\\frac13\\right)^6}{1-\\frac13}. $$\n\nNow,\n\n$$ \\left(\\frac13\\right)^6=\\frac{1}{729} $$\n\nand\n\n$$ 1-\\frac13=\\frac23. $$\n\nHence,\n\n$$ S_6=\\frac{1-\\frac{1}{729}}{\\frac23} =\\frac{\\frac{728}{729}}{\\frac23} =\\frac{728}{729}\\cdot \\frac32. $$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ S_6=\\frac{1092}{729} =\\frac{364}{243}. $$\n\nTherefore, the required sum is\n\n$$ \\boxed{\\frac{364}{243}}. $$\n\nSo the correct option is\n\n$$ \\boxed{\\text{Option C}}. $$",
