      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Evening Shift. Correct option: A. For the expansion of\n\n$$ \\left(9x-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{x}}\\right)^{18}, $$\n\nwe use the general term in the binomial expansion:\n\n$$ T_{r+1}=\\binom{18}{r}(9x)^{18-r}\\left(-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{x}}\\right)^r $$\n\nNow simplify the power of $x$ in this term.\n\nSince\n\n$$ (9x)^{18-r}=9^{18-r}x^{18-r} $$\n\nand\n\n$$ \\left(\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{x}}\\right)^r=\\frac{1}{3^r x^{r/2}}, $$\n\nso the power of $x$ in the general term is\n\n$$ x^{18-r-\\frac r2}=x^{18-\\frac{3r}{2}}. $$\n\nFor the term independent of $x$, the power of $x$ must be zero:\n\n$$ 18-\\frac{3r}{2}=0 $$\n\n$$ \\frac{3r}{2}=18 $$\n\n$$ r=12 $$\n\nSo the required term is the $(12+1)$th term, i.e. $T_{13}$.\n\nNow compute it:\n\n$$ T_{13}=\\binom{18}{12}(9x)^6\\left(-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{x}}\\right)^{12} $$\n\nSince the power of $x$ becomes zero, only the numerical coefficient remains:\n\n$$ T_{13}=\\binom{18}{12}\\cdot 9^6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3^{12}} $$\n\nAlso,\n\n$$ 9^6=(3^2)^6=3^{12} $$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ T_{13}=\\binom{18}{12} $$\n\nand\n\n$$ \\binom{18}{12}=\\binom{18}{6}=\\frac{18!}{6!\\,12!}=18564 $$\n\nGiven that the term independent of $x$ is $(221)k$, we have\n\n$$ 221k=18564 $$\n\nThus,\n\n$$ k=\\frac{18564}{221}=84 $$\n\nSo the correct answer is:\n\n$$ \\boxed{84} $$\n\nHence, Option A is correct.",
