      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 6th April Evening Shift. Correct option: A. Since the centre of the ellipse is at the origin and one directrix is $x=9$, the major axis is along the $x$-axis.\n\nLet the ellipse be\n\n$$ \\frac{x^2}{a^2}+\\frac{y^2}{b^2}=1 $$\n\nwith eccentricity\n\n$$ e=\\frac{1}{3} $$\n\nFor an ellipse with major axis along the $x$-axis, the directrices are\n\n$$ x=\\pm \\frac{a}{e} $$\n\nGiven one directrix is $x=9$, so\n\n$$ \\frac{a}{e}=9 $$\n\nSince $e=\\frac13$,\n\n$$ a=9\\cdot \\frac13=3 $$\n\nAlso,\n\n$$ c=ae=3\\cdot \\frac13=1 $$\n\nSo the focus $P(\\alpha,0)$ is\n\n$$ P=(1,0) $$\n\nHence $\\alpha=1$.\n\nNow,\n\n$$ b^2=a^2(1-e^2)=9\\left(1-\\frac19\\right)=9\\cdot \\frac89=8 $$\n\nTherefore the ellipse is\n\n$$ \\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{8}=1 $$\n\nLet the midpoint of chord $AB$ passing through the focus $(1,0)$ be $(h,k)$.\n\nFor the ellipse\n\n$$ \\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{8}=1 $$\n\nthe equation of the chord whose midpoint is $(h,k)$ is given by the NCERT midpoint formula:\n\n$$ \\frac{xx_1}{9}+\\frac{yy_1}{8}=\\frac{x_1^2}{9}+\\frac{y_1^2}{8} $$\n\nSo here the chord with midpoint $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$ \\frac{xh}{9}+\\frac{yk}{8}=\\frac{h^2}{9}+\\frac{k^2}{8} $$\n\nSince this chord passes through the focus $(1,0)$, substitute $(x,y)=(1,0)$:\n\n$$ \\frac{h}{9}=\\frac{h^2}{9}+\\frac{k^2}{8} $$\n\nMultiply by $72$:\n\n$$ 8h=8h^2+9k^2 $$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ 9k^2=8h-8h^2=8h(1-h) $$\n\nReplacing $(h,k)$ by $(x,y)$ for the locus of the midpoint, we get\n\n$$ 9y^2=8x(1-x) $$\n\nHence the required locus is\n\n$$ \\boxed{9y^2=8x(1-x)} $$\n\nSo the correct option is A .",
