      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Evening Shift. Correct option: D. Centroid :\n\nLet $Q$ be a point on the given circle. First write the circle in standard form:\n\n$$x^2+y^2-14x-14y+82=0 \\Rightarrow (x-7)^2+(y-7)^2=16$$\n\nSo we can take\n\n$$Q(7+4\\cos\\theta,\\;7+4\\sin\\theta)$$\n\nPoint $R$ lies on the line $x+y=5$. Let its ordinate be $y$, then its abscissa is $5-y$, so\n\n$$R(5-y,\\;y)$$\n\nNow use the centroid formula. For triangle $PQR$, centroid is\n\n$$ \\left(\\frac{x_P+x_Q+x_R}{3},\\;\\frac{y_P+y_Q+y_R}{3}\\right) $$\n\nGiven $P(3\\cos\\alpha,\\;2\\sin\\alpha)$, so the centroid becomes\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} & \\left(\\frac{3 \\cos \\alpha+7+4 \\cos \\theta+5-y}{3}, \\frac{2 \\sin \\alpha+7+4 \\sin \\theta+y}{3}\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(\\cos \\alpha+2, \\frac{2}{3} \\sin \\alpha+3\\right) \\end{aligned} $$\n\n[Diagram: JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Evening Shift Mathematics - Ellipse Question 2 English Explanation]\n\nOn comparison\n\nCompare the $x$-coordinates:\n\n$$\\frac{3 \\cos \\alpha+7+4 \\cos \\theta+5-y}{3}=\\cos\\alpha+2 \\Rightarrow 4\\cos\\theta=y-6$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ \\cos \\theta=\\frac{y-6}{4} $$\n\nCompare the $y$-coordinates:\n\n$$\\frac{2 \\sin \\alpha+7+4 \\sin \\theta+y}{3}=\\frac{2}{3}\\sin\\alpha+3 \\Rightarrow 4\\sin\\theta=2-y$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ \\sin \\theta=\\frac{2-y}{4} $$\n\nNow use the identity $\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta=1$:\n\n$$\\sin ^2 \\theta+\\cos ^2 \\theta=1 \\Rightarrow(y-6)^2+(y-2)^2=16$$\n\nSimplify:\n\n$$y^2-8 y+12=0$$\n\nThis quadratic gives two possible values of $y$ (ordinates of $R$). The sum of roots is $8$, so\n\n$$\\Rightarrow \\text { Sum of ordinates }=8$$",
