      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Evening Shift. Correct option: B. The given equation of the hyperbola is:\n\n$$ \\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1 $$\n\nLet the uniform eccentricity of the hyperbola be $e$.\n\nAccording to the problem, the distance between the foci is $6$. For a standard hyperbola, the coordinates of the foci are $(\\pm ae, 0)$, so the distance between them is given by $2ae$.\n\n$$ 2ae = 6 $$\n\n$$ ae = 3 \\quad \\dots (i) $$\n\nThe distance between the directrices is given as $\\frac{8}{3}$. The equations of the directrices are $x = \\pm \\frac{a}{e}$, so the distance between them is $2\\frac{a}{e}$.\n\n$$ 2\\frac{a}{e} = \\frac{8}{3} $$\n\n$$ \\frac{a}{e} = \\frac{4}{3} \\quad \\dots (ii) $$\n\nTo find the value of $a^2$, we can multiply equations (i) and (ii):\n\n$$ (ae) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{a}{e}\\right) = 3 \\times \\frac{4}{3} $$\n\n$$ a^2 = 4 $$\n\nWe know the fundamental relationship between $a, b,$ and $e$ for a hyperbola is:\n\n$$ b^2 = a^2(e^2 - 1) $$\n\n$$ b^2 = a^2e^2 - a^2 $$\n\nUsing the value $ae = 3$, we have $a^2e^2 = 9$. Substituting $a^2e^2$ and $a^2$ into the equation gives:\n\n$$ b^2 = 9 - 4 = 5 $$\n\nNow, substituting $a^2$ and $b^2$ back into the standard equation, the hyperbola is:\n\n$$ \\frac{x^2}{4} - \\frac{y^2}{5} = 1 $$\n\nThe line $x = \\alpha$ intersects the hyperbola at points A and B. Substitute $x = \\alpha$ into the equation of the hyperbola to find the y-coordinates of points A and B:\n\n$$ \\frac{\\alpha^2}{4} - \\frac{y^2}{5} = 1 $$\n\n$$ \\frac{y^2}{5} = \\frac{\\alpha^2}{4} - 1 $$\n\n$$ \\frac{y^2}{5} = \\frac{\\alpha^2 - 4}{4} $$\n\n$$ y^2 = \\frac{5(\\alpha^2 - 4)}{4} $$\n\n$$ y = \\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5(\\alpha^2 - 4)}}{2} $$\n\nSo, the coordinates of points A and B are $\\left(\\alpha, \\frac{\\sqrt{5(\\alpha^2 - 4)}}{2}\\right)$ and $\\left(\\alpha, -\\frac{\\sqrt{5(\\alpha^2 - 4)}}{2}\\right)$.\n\nThese points lie vertically above and below each other, forming the base of $\\triangle AOB$ along the line $x = \\alpha$.\n\nLength of the base $AB$ = Distance between A and B = $2 \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{5(\\alpha^2 - 4)}}{2} = \\sqrt{5(\\alpha^2 - 4)}$.\n\nThe perpendicular height of $\\triangle AOB$ from the origin $O(0,0)$ to the line $x = \\alpha$ is simply $|\\alpha|$.\n\nWe are given that the area of $\\triangle AOB$ is $4\\sqrt{15}$. Using the area of a triangle formula:\n\n$$ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} $$\n\n$$ 4\\sqrt{15} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{5(\\alpha^2 - 4)} \\cdot |\\alpha| $$\n\nSquaring both sides to remove the square root and the absolute value:\n\n$$ (4\\sqrt{15})^2 = \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot (\\alpha^2) \\cdot 5(\\alpha^2 - 4) $$\n\n$$ 240 = \\frac{5}{4} \\alpha^2 (\\alpha^2 - 4) $$\n\n$$ 240 \\times \\frac{4}{5} = \\alpha^2(\\alpha^2 - 4) $$\n\n$$ 192 = \\alpha^4 - 4\\alpha^2 $$\n\n$$ \\alpha^4 - 4\\alpha^2 - 192 = 0 $$\n\nLet $\\alpha^2 = t$. This transforms our equation into a quadratic equation:\n\n$$ t^2 - 4t - 192 = 0 $$\n\nSplitting the middle term:\n\n$$ t^2 - 16t + 12t - 192 = 0 $$\n\n$$ t(t - 16) + 12(t - 16) = 0 $$\n\n$$ (t - 16)(t + 12) = 0 $$\n\nSo, $t = 16$ or $t = -12$.\n\nSince $t = \\alpha^2$ and the square of a real number cannot be negative, we disregard $-12$.\n\nThus, we have:\n\n$$ \\alpha^2 = 16 $$\n\nOption B is the correct answer.",
