      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 6th April Morning Shift. Correct option: C. For the quadratic equation\n\n$$ x^2-ax+2=0 $$\n\nlet its two distinct roots be $e_1$ and $e_2$.\n\nWe are given that these roots are to be interpreted as eccentricities.\n\nRecall:\n\nFor a hyperbola, eccentricity $e>1$\n\nFor an ellipse, eccentricity satisfies $0\nAlso, since $e_1,e_2$ are roots of the quadratic, by Vieta's formulas,\n\n$$ e_1+e_2=a,\\qquad e_1e_2=2 $$\n\nand since the roots are distinct real roots,\n\n$$ a^2-8>0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad |a|>2\\sqrt2 $$\n\nNow we analyse the two sets.\n\n1. Both $e_1$ and $e_2$ are eccentricities of hyperbolas\n\nThis means both roots must satisfy\n\n$$ e_1>1,\\qquad e_2>1 $$\n\nSince\n\n$$ e_1e_2=2>0 $$\n\nthe two roots have the same sign.\n\nIf both are hyperbola eccentricities, both must be positive and greater than $1$.\n\nNow, for both roots of\n\n$$ x^2-ax+2=0 $$\n\nto be greater than $1$, we use the NCERT-style condition:\n\nFor both roots to be greater than $1$, we need\n\nroots real and distinct:\n\n$$ a^2-8>0 $$\n\nsum positive:\n\n$$ a>0 $$\n\nvalue of polynomial at $x=1$ positive, because $1$ must lie to the left of both roots:\n\n$$ f(1)=1-a+2=3-a>0 \\text{ or }\nLet us be careful.\n\nSince the parabola opens upward, if both roots are greater than $1$, then $x=1$ lies outside the roots on the left side, so\n\n$$ f(1)>0 $$\n\nHence,\n\n$$ 3-a>0 \\Rightarrow a Also, for both roots to be greater than $1$, the axis of symmetry should be to the right of $1$:\n\n$$ \\frac a2>1 \\Rightarrow a>2 $$\n\nCombining:\n\n$$ 2 But also we need distinct real roots, and in this interval automatically $a>2\\sqrt2$ is needed. Hence\n\n$$ 2\\sqrt2 So,\n\n$$ (\\alpha,\\beta)=(2\\sqrt2,\\,3) $$\n\nThus,\n\n$$ \\alpha=2\\sqrt2,\\qquad \\beta=3 $$\n\n2. $e_1$ is eccentricity of an ellipse and $e_2$ is eccentricity of a hyperbola, respectively\n\nThis means one root lies in $(0,1)$ and the other lies in $(1,\\infty)$.\n\nSince\n\n$$ e_1e_2=2>0 $$\n\nboth roots are positive. So the only possibility is:\n\none root in $(0,1)$\n\nthe other root greater than $1$\n\nNow let the roots be distinct and positive. Since their product is $2$, if one root is less than $1$, the other must automatically be greater than $2$, hence greater than $1$.\n\nSo we only need one root to be less than $1$.\n\nFor that, $x=1$ must lie between the two roots. Since the parabola opens upward, this happens when\n\n$$ f(1) That is,\n\n$$ 1-a+2 $$ 3-a $$ a>3 $$\n\nAlso for real distinct roots, $a^2-8>0$, which is already true for $a>3$.\n\nHence the set is\n\n$$ (\\gamma,\\infty)=(3,\\infty) $$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ \\gamma=3 $$\n\n3. Find $\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2$\n\nNow,\n\n$$ \\alpha^2=(2\\sqrt2)^2=8 $$\n\n$$ \\beta^2=3^2=9 $$\n\n$$ \\gamma^2=3^2=9 $$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$ \\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2=8+9+9=26 $$\n\nSo the correct answer is\n\n$$ \\boxed{26} $$\n\nHence, Option C .",
