      "a": 1,
      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 24th January Evening Shift. Correct option: B. Step 1: Write $f(x)$ without absolute values for different intervals\n\nWhen $x \\geq 1$, $\\ln x$ is positive and $x - 1$ is also positive. So:\n\n$$ f(x) = |\\ln x| - |x-1| = \\ln x - (x - 1) = \\ln x - x + 1 $$\n\nfor $x \\geq 1$.\n\nWhen $0\n\n$$ f(x) = |\\ln x| - |x-1| = -\\ln x - (-(x - 1)) = -\\ln x + x - 1 $$\n\nfor $0 So we can write:\n\n$$ f(x) = \\begin{cases} \\ln x - x + 1 & \\text{for } x \\geq 1 \\\\\\\\ -\\ln x + x - 1 & \\text{for } 0 Step 2: Find the derivative $f'(x)$ in both cases\n\nFor $x \\geq 1,$\n\n$$ f'(x) = \\frac{d}{dx} (\\ln x - x + 1) = \\frac{1}{x} - 1 $$\n\nFor $0\n\n$$ f'(x) = \\frac{d}{dx} (-\\ln x + x - 1) = -\\frac{1}{x} + 1 $$\n\nSo,\n\n$$ f'(x) = \\begin{cases} \\frac{1}{x} - 1 & \\text{for } x \\geq 1 \\\\\\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} + 1 & \\text{for } 0 Step 3: Check differentiability at $x = 1$\n\nFrom the left of $x = 1$:\n\n$$ f'(1^-) = -\\frac{1}{1} + 1 = 0 $$\n\nFrom the right of $x = 1$:\n\n$$ f'(1^+) = \\frac{1}{1} - 1 = 0 $$\n\nBoth one-sided derivatives are equal. So $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x = 1$.\n\nSince the formulas for $f'(x)$ are valid everywhere for $x > 0$, $f(x)$ is differentiable for all $x > 0$.\n\nStep 4: Check if $f(x)$ is increasing or decreasing\n\nFor $x > 1$:\n\n$$ f'(x) = \\frac{1}{x} - 1 $$\n\nSince $x > 1$, $\\frac{1}{x} 1$.\n\nFor $0 1$, so $-\\frac{1}{x} + 1 Step 5: Check the statements\n\n(I) $f$ is differentiable at all $x > 0$: True\n\n(II) $f$ is increasing in $(0, 1)$: False\n\n(III) $f$ is decreasing in $(1, \\infty)$: True\n\nSo only statements (I) and (III) are correct.",
