      "s": "JEE Main 2026 (Online) 4th April Morning Shift. Correct option: B. Let the given integral be denoted by $I$. We can split $I$ into two separate integrals:\n\n$$I = \\int\\limits_{-2}^2 (|\\sin x| + [x \\sin x]) dx = \\int\\limits_{-2}^2 |\\sin x| dx + \\int\\limits_{-2}^2 [x \\sin x] dx$$\n\nLet $I_1 = \\int\\limits_{-2}^2 |\\sin x| dx$ and $I_2 = \\int\\limits_{-2}^2 [x \\sin x] dx$.\n\nStep 1: Evaluate $I_1$\n\nBased on the property of definite integrals, if $f(x)$ is an even function (i.e., $f(-x) = f(x)$), then $\\int_{-a}^a f(x) dx = 2 \\int_0^a f(x) dx$.\n\nSince $|\\sin(-x)| = |-\\sin x| = |\\sin x|$, the function is even. Thus,\n\n$$I_1 = 2 \\int\\limits_0^2 |\\sin x| dx$$\n\nIn the interval $[0, 2]$, $2$ radians is less than $\\pi \\ (\\approx 3.14)$, so $\\sin x > 0$. Hence, $|\\sin x| = \\sin x$.\n\n$$I_1 = 2 \\int\\limits_0^2 \\sin x dx$$\n\n$$I_1 = 2 [-\\cos x]_0^2 = 2 (-\\cos 2 - (-\\cos 0))$$\n\n$$I_1 = 2 (1 - \\cos 2) = 2 - 2 \\cos 2$$\n\nStep 2: Evaluate $I_2$\n\nLet $f(x) = x \\sin x$. We check if it is even or odd:\n\n$f(-x) = (-x) \\sin(-x) = (-x) (-\\sin x) = x \\sin x = f(x)$.\n\nSince $f(x)$ is an even function, $[x \\sin x]$ is also an even function.\n\n$$I_2 = 2 \\int\\limits_0^2 [x \\sin x] dx$$\n\nNow, let us analyze the behavior of $f(x) = x \\sin x$ in the interval $x \\in [0, 2]$:\n\nWhen $x = 0$, $f(0) = 0$.\n\nWhen $x = 2$, $f(2) = 2 \\sin 2$.\n\nNotice that $\\pi/2 So, $1.732 Let's verify if $f(x)$ is strictly increasing in $[0, 2]$. We differentiate $f(x)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$f'(x) = \\sin x + x \\cos x$$\n\nFor $x \\in (0, \\pi/2]$, both $\\sin x > 0$ and $\\cos x \\ge 0$, so $f'(x) > 0$.\n\nFor $x \\in (\\pi/2, 2]$, $\\cos x $$f'(x) = \\cos x (\\tan x + x)$$\n\nSince $\\tan x$ is negative in the second quadrant and $2$ radians is smaller than $\\tan^{-1}(-2)$, the expression $(\\tan x + x)$ evaluates to a negative value. Therefore, $\\cos x$ (negative) $\\times$ $(\\tan x + x)$ (negative) will yield $f'(x) > 0$.\n\nSince $f(x)$ strictly increases from $0$ to a value between $1$ and $2$, there is exactly one real number $\\alpha \\in (0, 2)$ at which $f(\\alpha) = 1$. So,\n\n$$\\alpha \\sin \\alpha = 1$$\n\nFor $x \\in [0, \\alpha)$, the function values are $0 \\le x \\sin x For $x \\in [\\alpha, 2]$, the function values are $1 \\le x \\sin x Now, split the integral $I_2$ at $x = \\alpha$:\n\n$$I_2 = 2 \\left( \\int\\limits_0^\\alpha 0 dx + \\int\\limits_\\alpha^2 1 dx \\right)$$\n\n$$I_2 = 2 [x]_\\alpha^2 = 2(2 - \\alpha) = 4 - 2\\alpha$$\n\nStep 3: Finding the value of $\\beta \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)$\n\nCombining $I_1$ and $I_2$, we get the total integral:\n\n$$I = I_1 + I_2 = (2 - 2 \\cos 2) + (4 - 2\\alpha) = 6 - 2 \\cos 2 - 2\\alpha$$\n\n$$I = 2(3 - \\cos 2) - 2\\alpha$$\n\nAccording to the question, the total integral evaluates to:\n\n$$I = 2(3 - \\cos 2) + \\beta$$\n\nComparing our result with the given equation, we find:\n\n$$\\beta = -2\\alpha$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\beta = -2\\alpha$ into the expression $\\beta \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)$:\n\n$$\\beta \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) = -2\\alpha \\cdot \\sin\\left(\\frac{-2\\alpha}{2}\\right)$$\n\n$$\\beta \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) = -2\\alpha \\cdot \\sin(-\\alpha) = -2\\alpha (-\\sin \\alpha)$$\n\n$$\\beta \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) = 2 (\\alpha \\sin \\alpha)$$\n\nFrom our earlier deduction in Step 2, we established that $\\alpha \\sin \\alpha = 1$. Therefore:\n\n$$\\beta \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) = 2 (1) = 2$$\n\nOption B is the correct answer.",
